'''Castejón''' ({{Audio|bl-Castejón.ogg|pronunciation}}, meaning "small castle"; {{Langx|an|Castillón|label=|engvar=romanized}}; {{Langx|gug|Apúchína|4=gathering place}}), officially the '''Metropolitan City of Castejón''' ({{lang-bl|Ciudad Metropolitana de Castejón}}; {{Langx|gug|Táva-Guasu Apúchína}}), known simply as the '''Castejón Metropolis''' ({{lang-bl|Metrópoli de Castejón}}) is the capital and largest city of the [[Balisca|Baliscan Confederation]]. The city makes up the central portion of the [[Alpujarra]] in the south-east region of the [[Levante, Balisca|Levante]]. With a [[Regions of Balisca|regional]] population over thirty-three million people in 2026, Castejón is the most-populous metropolitan area in the Baliscan Confederation, [[Abyala]] and the [[Western Hemisphere]], and one of the [[List of largest cities|largest in the world]]. An [[Globalization and World Cities Ranks|Alpha+ global city]], Castejón has risen to become a center of cuisine, fashion, business, and contemporary arts in the Algarves. In addition to being a highly [[W:Polyculturalism|polycultural]] area, it is one of the world’s most livable cities. It is known for its preserved [[w:eclecticism|eclectic]] [[#Architecture|architecture]] and rich [[w:culture|cultural life]]. Castejón is a {{W|cosmopolitanism|cosmopolitan}} metropolis that is home to hundreds of ethnic and religious groups, contributing to its culture as well as to the [[Castejón dialect|dialect spoken in the city]] and in some other parts of the country. This is because since the 19th century, the city (and the country in general), has been a major recipient of millions of [[Immigration to Balisca|immigrants from all over the world]], gradually forming a {{W|cultural mosaic}}.

Until 1836, the loosely coupled confederation did not have a central political organization. Issues thought to affect the whole Confederation were the subject of periodic meetings in various locations. The [[Articles of Confederation]] (1810) provided few details concerning institutions, and this was not discussed until the [[1826 Confederal Congress]] in Alessandria, but was largely ineffective, as all [[Bayara|Bayaran]] regions except [[Carpinchera Republic|Carpinchera]] boycotted the congress. The [[Baliscan Civil War|Bayara secession]] and mobilization against the Congress led to an early termination without an definitive agreement. Around the same time, Castejón was considered to be the {{W|frontier}} of the fledgling confederation, a settlement in the "[[La Frontera|middle ground]]" in between cultures, peoples, and beyond state control. It gradually came to be called the "place of no gods", as it was suspiciously viewed as an aberration by many both before and after independence due to its flamboyant and cosmopolitan culture. Bayaran armies conducted an ill-fated campaign to capture the Alpujarra in 1827, which culminated in the [[Siege of Castejón]] and saw the intervention of the [[Maroon Confederacy]] on the side of the Confederalists. After years of debate, shifting demographics, and negotiations and [[Concordia Accords|reconciliation]] between the confederations of Indigenous peoples, the Maroon Confederacy, and the post-colonial [[Communities of Balisca|republics]], the [[1836 Confederal Convention]] symbolically recognized Castejón as the "cradle of the confederation", establishing the city as the ''de facto'' capital.

The city is the most substantial economic engine within the nation, with a diverse economy in a broad range of professional and cultural fields. 25% of the city is designated or protected [[W:Green space|green space]], one of the world's highest proportions. It's economy is based on high-tech firms and the [[W:Service sector|service sector]], encompassing a diverse range of creative industries, research facilities, media cooperatives and convention venues. Significant industries also include [[W:IT|IT]], pharmaceuticals, [[W:Biomedical engineering|biomedical engineering]], [[W:Clean tech|cleantech]], [[W:Biotechnology|biotechnology]], construction and [[W:Software engineering|software engineering]]. Today, the city is known as the heart of the Baliscan tech industry due to the concentration of tech companies, {{W|alternative finance}}, consumer electronics developers, and gaming corporations. Castejón has emerged as a global pioneer into {{W|adaptive reuse}}, {{W|urban regeneration}}, {{W|Drawdown (climate)|climate drawdown}} and {{W|economic degrowth}}. The city has consistently ranked highly as one of the world’s most livable, reflecting its commitment to the {{W|compact city|compact}} and {{W|15-minute city}} concepts, {{W|district heating}}, [[Castejón Metropolitan Rapid Transit|well-developed public transportation systems]], comprehensive [[Social protection in Balisca|social protection]] policies, and a [[Baliscan values|progressive cultural and political climate]]. 

Although most of the world recognizes Castejón as a city, since 1920 its governing structure has been more akin to that of a [[Regions of Balisca|region]], with an accompanying [[Metropolitan Council of Castejón|legislature]] taking precedence over the smaller communal governments that make up the metropolis. The term "Castejón Metropolis" is coterminous with the "Castejón Region". The process of {{W|urban sprawl}} between the densely populated city and the immediately surrounding {{W|satellite city|satellite cities}} has formed the [[Alpujarra Megalopolis|Alpujarras Megalopolis]] ([[Valparaíso]], [[Amposta]], [[Yerba Buena]], and several smaller communes). Continued population growth around this region has additionally created one continuous {{W|conurbation}} named [[Concordia]] (Baliscano: “concord” or “harmony”) stretching from the plains of [[Tukupi]] to the coast of [[Luzamontaña]].

==History==
{{Main|History of Castejón}}

===Indigenous settlement===
{{align|left|{{Quote box|width=20em|align=left|bgcolor=#B0C4DE|title=Historical affiliations|fontsize=90%|quote={{flagicon|Volisania}} [[United Kingdoms of Volisania|Volisan Empire]] 1533–1707<br/>{{flagicon image|War Flag of the Maroon Confederacy.svg |border}} [[Maroon Confederacy]]  1707–1818<br/>{{flag|Northern Confederation}} 1818–1836<br/>{{flagicon|Balisca}} [[Balisca|Baliscan Confederation]] 1836–present}}}}

[[File:Quoniambec.jpg|left|thumb|243x243px|[[Irapirangu]], an indigenous chieftain of the [[Kuarupíba]] tribe that resisted early European exploration of the [[Alpujarra]].]]
Various [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|indigenous peoples]] have a rich and ancient history in the [[Alpujarra]], which is now part of the modern city of Castejon. This region, nestled in the picturesque valley amongst the [[Matarrañya|Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]], has been inhabited by various indigenous communities for thousands of years. Among these communities, the [[Kuarupíba]], a branch of [[Kurupiranga]] (a [[W:Tupi people|Kurupiran people]] descended from [[Pindorama]]) have played a significant role in shaping the cultural and historical tapestry of the region.

The presence of the people in the [[Alpujarra]] can be traced back to [[W:Prehistory|prehistoric times]], with evidence of human habitation in the Alpujarra dating back to the Paleolithic era. Archaeological discoveries, such as [[W:Cave paintings|cave paintings]] and stone tools, attest to the presence of early human settlements in the area. The Kuarupíba people are known for their remarkable craftsmanship, creating intricate pottery and decorative objects that showcased both artistic abilities and advanced techniques.

As the centuries passed, the Kuarupíba established a well-structured society with a distinct cultural identity. They developed a complex system of governance, based on communal decision-making and a deep connection with their natural surroundings. From 1438 to 1533, the Kuarupíba were dispersed across a large portion of the eastern coast of Abyala known to them as ''Yby-Pirirí'' ({{Literal translation|land of wind}}), settling along the [[Matarrañya|Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]], utilizing both violent conquest and peaceful assimilation, among other methods. At its largest, pre-colonial settlement of the Kuarupíba extended as far south as the [[Strait of Ivorra]].

Notable features of the pre-colonial society in the Alpujarra included {{W|terrace farm}}ing, {{W|stonework}}, extensive road network crossing mountains and valleys, finely-woven textiles, use of knotted strings for record keeping and communication, agricultural innovations and production in a difficult environment, and the organization and management fostered or imposed on its people and their labor. The surrounding area provided abundant natural resources, including fertile land for agriculture, forests for hunting, and rivers teeming with fish. They additionally mastered sustainable agricultural practices, cultivating crops such as corn, beans, and squash, which formed the basis of their diet.
====Andalusi colonial efforts====
{{Main|Colonial Balisca}}

[[File:Landing at La Baba.png.png|thumb|300x300px|''Arribando a La Baba'' by [[Jonatan Freixas]], depicts the arrival of [[Fructuós Oriol Juanico]]'s fleet at [[La Baba]], becoming the first Europeans to enter [[Salto Bay]]|left]]
[[United Kingdoms of Volisania|Volisania]]—before that, the [[Andalusi League]]—had for a long time been sending their convicts across the [[Aethiopic Ocean]] to the Abyalan colonies. This was significantly expanded during the [[Andalusi rebellion of 1508]]. In 1515, navigator and explorer [[Fructuós Oriol Juanico]] navigating in the name of the Volisanian Crown, was the first Illypnian to enter [[Salto Bay]]. His expedition was cut short when he was [[Battle of Takuara|killed during an attack]] by the local [[Kuarupíba]] tribe in what is now the city's [[Charxes]] borough. 

[[Ambròs Joaquim Almazán]], an Andalusi [[Mudéjar]] convicted and expelled from Andalus, led a group of around 80 escaped convicts south across the [[Cerroitá Pass]], arriving at what is now [[Limón]] on 18 February 1533. The expedition deemed the location unsuitable due to the hostile reaction of the indigenous tribes to their arrival. The first settlement not established by indigenous people's within what is now Castejón was ''Guardafui'' ({{Literal translation|Look and escape}}). The settlement founded by Almazán was located in what is today the [[Commune of Castejón|''Comuna Central'']] barrio of [[Ampurias]]. The settlers later erected a 12-foot wooden {{W|stockade}} in 1534 to protect against raids by the Indigenous tribes. The settlement was one of many that was not founded on the initiative of any colonial authority in Northern Abyala. Maps from this time show no prison buildings; the punishment for convicts was typically hard labor rather than incarceration, but serious offenses were penalized by [[W:Flogging|flogging]], [[W:Expulsion|expulsion]] or death. Almazán sent exploratory missions in search of better soils and fixed on the western region as a promising area for expansion and moved many of the convicts from late 1568 to establish a [[Gistaín|small township]], which became the main centre of the colony's economic life, leaving Salto Bay only as an anchorage and point of trade with friendly indigenous peoples.

Poor equipment and unfamiliar soils and climate continued to hamper the expansion of farming, but a building programme, assisted by convict labour, advanced steadily. Guardafui's status as a [[La Frontera (Balisca)|frontier settlement]] meant that it was largely outside the control of the Volisanian Crown leading to an increasingly widespread anti-royalist sentiment. From its earliest days, Castejón depended primarily on piracy and trade with the well-established indigenous communities of the Alpujarra, before shifting to illegal smuggling and ties with the [[Hesperian League]] as the Illypnians consolidated their footholds in Abyala. During most of the 18th century, Mesogean ships were [[Maroon piracy|menaced by raiders]], often originating from [[Salto Bay]] and the [[Maroon Confederacy]].  

===Baliscan independence and civil war===
{{Main|Volisanya Boba|Baliscan War of Independence|}}

{{W|Napoleon I}}, the Emperor of France, forced the King of {{W|History of Spain (1808–1874)|Spain}}, [[W:Charles IV of Spain|Charles VI]] and his son, [[W:Ferdinand VII of Spain|Ferdinand VII]], to renounce the Spanish throne in favor of himself in the {{W|Abdications of Bayonne}}. Napoleon in his turn, handed the throne of Spain to his brother, {{W|Joseph Bonaparte}}. Although the Spanish world almost uniformly rejected Napoleon's plan to place his brother on the throne, there was no clear solution to the lack of a king.

Following traditional Spanish political theories on the contractual nature of the monarchy (see {{W|Philosophy of Law of Francisco Suárez}}), the peninsular Spanish provinces responded to the crisis by establishing juntas. The move, however, led to more confusion, since there was no central authority and most juntas did not recognize the claim of some juntas to represent the monarchy as a whole. The {{W|Junta of Seville}}, in particular, claimed authority over the overseas empire, because of the province's historic role as the exclusive {{W|entrepôt}} of the empire.

On 4 May 1809, ''[[Volisanyado|Volisanyados]]'' in Castejón attempted to seize control of the settlement and declare their loyalty to the unified [[Community of Volisania|Volisan Community]]. Royalists in Castejón formed a relatively small minority, mostly consisting of Volisanian and Beron-speaking Catholics, while being overshadowed by those who were largely apathetic or hostile to the plight of both the Volisans and the fledgling republics. Royalist sympathizers, typically traders whose wealth and influence had declined since the [[Volisanya Boba|uncoupling from imperial rule]], placed their hopes not in the seemingly illusory promises of fair treatment by the new regime, but rather in the rewards and wealth they could secure through fidelity to it.
[[File:El Tres de Mayo, by Francisco de Goya, from Prado in Google Earth-x0-y1.jpg|left|thumb|''Los Chuzios de Barcanal'' by [[Karim García]], depicting the execution of several Royalist conspirators]]
This attempted [[W:Coup|coup]] was violently crushed by the [[Defense Cabildo]] (consisting of a coalition of anti-royalists, Maroons, [[Hesperian League|Hesperian mercenaries]] and traders), resulting in the [[Trials of Barcanal]], on 17 May 1813. During the trial all 68 royalist conspirators were convicted of conspiring with the Volisanian Crown, and all were publicly executed shortly afterwards by [[W:Firing squad|firing squad]]. After this point, the city was openly {{W|free city|tolerant}}, if not supportive, towards refugees and dissident activity against the Volisanian Community, building upon the legacy of the first self-liberated slaves whom had transversed the Alpujarra on their way to the [[Maroon Confederacy|Maroon territories]]. 

Popular sentiment swiftly turned against the Volisans, shortly after the revolt was crushed. During the night of 16 September 1814, the [[Great Fire of Castejón]] broke out in what is now [[Ampurias]]. The fire destroyed about 10 to 25 percent of the buildings in the city, while some unaffected parts of the city were plundered. Many people believed or assumed that one or more people deliberately started the fire, for a variety of different reasons. The few royalist sympathizers that remaining accused the revolutionaries that were acting within the city, while many commoners blamed supposed royalist cells for starting the blaze as retribution for the executions.  
[[File:Refugees in Salto Bay.png|thumb|250px|right|''El Abrazu de Castejón'' by [[Xabier Sánchez]], depicting some of the thousands arriving in Salto Bay following the [[Congress of Mercaderes]].]]
These tensions came to a head again with the blowing up of the [[Volisan frigate Calatayud|Volisan frigate Calatayu]], anchored in Salto Bay, by anti-Imperial sympathizers in the summer of 1815. The decisive defeat of a Royalist army at the [[Battle of Barra Alta]] (southern Hesperia) prevented a potential royalist evacuation overland. This proved to be the final blow to the royalist movement in the [[Levante, Balisca|Levante]] as they faced potential {{W|encirclement}} within the Alpujarra. The royalists began their withdrawal from the city on 28 June 1816, eventually sailing down the [[Guadalquivir]] and out to sea on 3 July 1816. 

This event was one of the most significant in the ''[[Continuation War|Resistencía]]'', and many volunteers and [[W:Privateer|privateers]] left the region to fight the diverse strongholds of royalist resistance, with varying levels of success. The [[Cabildo of Castejón]] was convened for the first time on 10 July 1816, overwhelmingly consisting of republicans and their sympathizers. Formal alignment with the republican cause was declared by the [[Defense Cabdilo]] in on 20 July, at the [[Congress of Mercaderes]]. Castejón managed to endure the remainder of the [[Abyalan Wars of Independence]] without falling again under royalist threat. [[Salto Bay]] rapidly became the epicenter of a massive movement of refugees fleeing the advance of royalist forces, particuarly from Bayara.

==== Civil War and Siege of Castejon ====
{{Main|Baliscan Civil War|Siege of Castejón}}

The Bayaran secession and mobilization against the [[Southern Confederation|Confederal Congress]] led to it's early termination without an definitive agreement. Around the time of the outbreak of the [[Baliscan Civil War]], Castejón was considered to be the frontier of the fledgling confederation to the [[Maroon Confederacy]], effectively a settlement in the "middle ground" in between cultures, peoples, and beyond the control of any republic control. It had already come to be known as a "place of no god", as it was suspiciously viewed as an aberration by many both before and after independence due to its flamboyant and cosmopolitan culture.

The Bayaran armies invaded the [[Alpujarra]] in the spring of 1828, with the goal of gaining a mountain pass through which they would be able to directly threaten the Hesperian mainland and sever any potential land routes an invasion of Bayara itself. The Bayarans easily swept south and besieged the settlement of Castejón. The siege was broken on 23 March 1829 following the intervention of the [[Maroon Confederacy]] on the side of the Confederalists, and the Bayarans withdrew.

===Confederal district===
{{Main|District of Castejón}}

{{See also|Anyos Federales}}
[[File:1836 Confederal Convention.png|thumb|''Confederalismo'' (1845), depicting the convening of the [[1836 Confederal Convention]], after which Castejón would become the ''de facto'' capital of Balisca]]
Following the end of the [[Baliscan Civil War]], a [[confederal convention]] took place in Castejón beginning on 9 July 1836. The city and it's scenery made such a favorable impression on the delegates, that at the convention's end it was determined that Castejón would serve as a "middle ground" from which all [[Ani Federali|confederal]] entities could be concentrated. This would later be emerge as an autonomous "confederal district". 
{{quote|''Nos planta raices aqui en Castejón. La ciudad es la casa medio de gente oprimido de Mundo Viejo y Nuevo; la tierra comun de todo gente de mundo.''<br/>

"Let us plant our roots here in Castejón. The city is the half-way house of the downtrodden peoples of both the Old and New Worlds; the common ground of all the peoples of the world."|personquoted=Màxim Morató, [[Cerdanya]]n delegate to Confederal Convention}}

Two pre-existing settlements were included in the territory of the district: the port of [[Sástago]] in the northeast, founded in 1616, and the planned town of [[Amanar]], founded in 1621. During 1836–37, a team under Michi Esposito, including Esposito's brothers Aloys and Josep and [[Sanyan|Sanyanian]] astronomer [[Igwenagu Berreyarza]], surveyed the borders of the federal district and placed boundary stones at every kilometer point. Many of the stones are still standing throughout the city. A new urban plan for the confederal district called for the construction of a new barrio on the west bank of the Alagón, planned to provide accommodation to 50,000 refugees that had arrived from Bayara. A new urban plan for the confederal district called for the construction of a new canton on the west bank of the Alagón, planned to provide accommodation to 50,000 refugees that had arrived from Bayara. On 12 July 1837, the canton, named [[Coruxón]] (meaning "the owls") was founded.

The Confederal Congress held its first session in Ampurias on 4 October 1838, and on the same day, the [[District of Castejón]] came into being. The Confederal Congress would later adopt the [[District of Castejón Organic Act]] of 1840 which officially organized the district and placed the entire territory under the exclusive control of the confederal government.[[File:Almiserat.png|thumb|The town of [[Almiserat]] ({{Circa|1855}}), located in what is now [[Llanito]]]]The "District of Castejón" was established as an [[W:Autonomous territory|autonomous territory]], outside of the administration of any of the Baliscan republics. The "core" of the District were made up by the present-day [[Expansión]], [[Ampurias]] and [[Sástago]] boroughs, now known as the "core boroughs" of Castejón. The city developed in the post-civil war period as a market and trading city due to its advantageous location on the [[Alagón River|Alagón river]]. At the time the city was facing a large influx of refugees displaced during the fighting of the civil war. Heavily influenced by the reform minded [[Confederal Congress]], the groundwork for the expansion of Castejón into a {{W|compact city}} on a "{{W|complete streets|complete}} and {{W|human scale|humane}}" scale was gradually expanded. After the passage of this Act, citizens living in the district were no longer considered residents of any Baliscan republic, which therefore ended their representation in Congress. Following the confederal ratification of the [[Kafto-Ethiopian Charter]], the city received another influx of people, the vast majority being liberated Kaftians who were seeking passage to the [[Maroon Confederacy]]. Many of these migrants eventually settled near the outskirts of Castejón, establishing thriving communities, such as [[Llanito|Lyanítu]] (which was later absorbed into Castejón), and [[Yerba Buena]], along with some of the first [[W:Ethnic enclaves|ethnic enclaves]]. 

After years of debate, shifting demographics, and negotiations and [[Concordia Accords|reconciliation]] between the confederations of Indigenous peoples, [[Maroon Confederacy|Araucaria]], and the [[republics of Balisca]], the [[1883 Confederal Convention]] symbolically recognized Castejón as the "cradle of the confederation". This enshrined the de facto position of the city as the confederal capital. Simultaneously, Araucaria joined the Confederation, bringing the territory of the [[Northern Cone]] under one political entity for the first time. As a prototypical confederal compromise, the Congress agreed to the location of other administrative institutions, such as the ? (1854) to a diverse variety of communes across the Confederation.

===Immigration and expansion===
Beginning the the late 19th century, the city emerged as one of the major gateways into Balisca for incoming migrants. It's peripheral regions were progressively transformed by both commercial and residential development relating to its status as a confederal and international trading center, as well as by increasing immigration from the [[Mesogean world]], [[North Fosia|North]] and [[East Fosia]], respectively. The city adopted the [[Commissions' Plan of 1911]], which expanded the [[W:Street grid|street grid]] of [[Coruxón]] to create a new borough, [[Charxes]]. 
[[File:SS Tartessia Salto Narrows.png|thumb|330x330px|''[[Tricontinental Company|SS Tartessia]]'' passing the [[Salto Narrows]] in 1917, operating on the [[Tricontinental Company|Ampuria-Alessandria-Castejón route]]]]
[[File:La Baba immigration station.png|left|thumb|270x270px|[[La Baba Immigration Center]], now a [[W:historic site|preserved heritage site]], welcomed millions of immigrants to the [[Alpujarra]] ({{Circa|1914}})]]
However this era brought a number of societal issues that were too numerous for the communes and cantons to effectively confront. Housing and sanitation were the most pressing issues, and the the outer fringes at the time were particularly notorious for [[W:Informal settlement|informal settlements]], overcrowding and inadequate sewage management systems. The influx of people far outstripped the existing supply of housing and placed increased strain on the city's sanitation network, which was already inadequate for the existing population.  

Historically, settlements in the [[Alpujarra]] were concentrated on or around hills, and during the immigration wave of the 20th century, these communities remained overwhelmingly populated by Baliscan-born nationals. As a result many migrants were forced to live in areas on the downhill or on the peripheries (eventually coalescing into [[W:Slum|slums]]) where they faced unsanitary conditions, leading to outbreaks of diseases such as [[W:Cholera|cholera]] and [[W:Typhus|typhus]]. The inadequate transport network also led to traffic congestion and longer commuting times, further exacerbating the problems in the cities.  

In response to these issues, cooperative movements based on the concept of "[[W:do-ocracy|do-ocracy]]", in which they seize authority over these issues autonomously in the face of government inadequacy. This was often through consensus-driven communal meetings, coordinating their efforts to improve their living and working conditions of their communities. These broad movements later turned to wider cantonal and the confederal politics, establishing increased representation in the [[Metropolitan Council of Castejón|metropolitan]] and [[Confederal Council|confederal]] councils, from which they successfully pressed for laws that enshrined and protected the rights of migrants. They also pressured representatives for the continued expansion of the metropolitan transportation system to connect the burgeoning outskirts with the economically dominant nodes, eventually leading to the development of [[W:Rapid transit|rapid transit]] in the form the [[Castejón Metro]]. 

==== Revolution and its effects ====
{{See also|Red Summer|Tragic Week|1919 Baliscan coup attempt}}
[[File:Castejon uprising (1918).png|thumb|330x330px|[[Autonomous Militia|Anarchist militants]] standing at a barricade during the [[1919 Baliscan coup attempt|Castejón uprising]] amid the [[Baliscan Revolution]]]]As the [[1919 Confederal Convention]], it became the focal point for the tensions between the various factions of the revolutionary popular front. By this time, [[W:Anarcho-syndicalism|anarcho-syndicalist]] [[Trade unions in Balisca|unions]] had already established themselves as the de facto governing force in the city's key sectors, including its vital dockyards, transport networks, and printing presses. These unions, bolstered by a robust network of mutual aid societies and neighborhood assemblies, commanded widespread popular support, which contrasted sharply with the more rigid, top-down structure of the [[Communist Party of Balisca]].

This underlying tension erupted into open conflict in June 1919. Fearing their marginalization in the new constitutional order being drafted at the Convention, the [[W:Bolsheviks|Bolshevik]]-aligned faction of the Communist Party launched an armed coup attempt on June 13th. Their goal was to dissolve the Convention by force, seize control of Castejón's key infrastructure, and declare a centralized [[W:Socialist republic|socialist republic]]. The uprising transformed the city’s streets into a complex urban battlefield, with the communists' [[W:Red Guards|Red Guards]] establishing the rival [[Provisional Central Committee]] and attempting to hunt down and arrest anarchist delegates.

The coup was ultimately defeated by a swift and decisive response from the local [[Autonomous Militia|autonomous militias]]. Already mobilized from the events of the [[Tragic Week]], these decentralized defense forces, enjoying intimate knowledge of the city's meandering and narrow streets, effectively isolated and dismantled the communist insurrection. The week of intense urban fighting, now known as the [[May Days]], would fizzle out by the 20th of June, when the last of the communist holdouts had surrendered.

The effects of the failed coup on Castejón were profound and long-lasting. The event shattered the unity of the left and solidified the dominance of anarchist and libertarian socialist principles in the confederal political culture for decades to come. It fostered a profound and lasting suspicion of centralized, party-led politics across the confederation, leading to the adoption of the [[Basic Law: Political Parties, Organization and Representation]].

==== Consolidation and growth ====
In 1920 the metropolis was elevated to the status of an [[Communities of Balisca|community]], but with significantly more political competencies than it's counterparts. The catalyst for this transformation was set in motion during the the later years of the ''[[Cosmocazo]]'', characterized by an extended and intricate grassroots campaign, accompanied by a comprehensive array of public relations efforts spanning the entirety of the 1890s through the early 1910s. This persistent and well-coordinated endeavor laid the groundwork for a substantial shift in the administrative landscape, reaching its culmination with the formalization of the new [[Metropolitan Charter of Castejón|Metropolitan Charter of "Greater Castejón"]]. This legislative milestone played a central role in facilitating a significant consolidation, fundamentally reshaping the region's administrative framework.

The consolidation, as outlined in the aforementioned charter, bore the hallmarks of a comprehensive restructuring effort. The core boroughs situated in the valley were already well integrated with each other, albeit without a unified framework. This new jurisdiction now encompassed almost the entirety of the [[Salto Bay]], an expansion that significantly influenced the administrative construct.

Within this expanded jurisdiction, the metropolitan administration materialized in ten distinct boroughs. This integration, extending beyond mere administrative rearrangement, represented a paradigm shift, broadening and clarifying territorial boundaries for the first time, setting the stage for subsequent organizational and structural adaptations at the confederal level. In this reconfigured landscape emerged a seamlessly integrated governing body—a collaborative and cooperative administrative structure—now acknowledged as the contemporary Castejón. It is interchangeably denoted as ''Castejón Region'' or the ''Castejón Metropolis'', embodying not only an administrative metamorphosis but also it's cosmopolitan identity.
[[File:Tistaina Canal earthquake aftermath.png|left|thumb|Ruins along the [[Tistaina Canal]] in [[Mercadero]] in the aftermath of the [[1924 Alpujarra earthquake]]]]
In 1924 the Metropolis and it's surrounding areas were [[1924 Alpujarra earthquake|struck by a earthquake]] with a [[W:Moment magnitude scale|M<sub>w</sub>]] magnitude of 7.9, which killed over 58,000 people (mostly from the resulting conflagration) and injured over 220,000 more across the [[Alpujarra]], and caused substantial damage across the city. Almost immediately after the quake (and even during the disaster), planning and reconstruction plans were hatched to quickly rebuild the city. 

As the Metropolitan authorities attempted to rebuild the devastated capital city they initially drafted a comprehensive reconstruction plan, but soon concluded that they lacked the budget to carry it out. And so, in areas where neither the metropolitan government nor the country’s real-estate and transportation cooperatives could properly fund reconstruction efforts, whole neighborhoods instead rapidly rebuilt themselves. Working on a small scale, residents [[W:Building cooperative|rebuilt homes and shops using scraped-together funds while relying on little more than their collective grit and inventiveness]], and [[W:Black market|black markets]] full of micro-entrepreneurs sprung up around the city’s major train stations. These neighborhoods were not initially planned, per se—they ''emerged'', and their ramshackle, spontaneous spirit can still be felt today when walking Castejón's [[W:Alley|backstreets]]. 

This approach was adopted out of harsh necessity, but the resulting neighborhoods developed a striking charm: intimate townscapes with exceptional vitality and livability, featuring a fine-grained urban fabric comprised of numerous small buildings. Before long, Castejón's post-earthquake spontaneity gave way to more methodical planning measures designed to impose at least some measure of order on the city’s development, but in doing so they invested little effort in changing the character of these post-earthquake neighborhoods.

==== Estau Nuevo and the Baliscan miracle ====
{{Main|Estau Nuevo|Balisca in the Great Adonian War|Baliscan miracle}}

[[File:Salto Bay Bridge underconstruction.jpg|thumb|331x331px|The [[Salto Bay Bridge]] under construction in 1934, linking [[Ampurias]] and [[Trinacria]]]]Under the guidance of the [[Instituto por la Reconstrución Industrial|IRI]], Castejón's government, with its newly-gained metropolitan authority, initiated a massive public housing and infrastructure program. This included the construction of the [[Salto Bay Bridge]] (completed 1936), the expansion of transport networks, and the construction of numerous public buildings. These projects not only provided work (and thus relief from the economic uncertainty of the Great Depression) but also began to methodically address the city's persistent sanitation, housing, and infrastructure issues.

Balisca's entry into the [[Great Adonian War]] in 1939 transformed this public works agenda into a full wartime mobilization. The war, which saw over 600,000 Baliscans serve, spurred a massive expansion of Castejón's industrial base; Shipyards on [[Salto Bay]] (like [[Astillero Naval]]), factories, and arsenals were rapidly developed or repurposed for the war effort, attracting a new wave of internal and international migrants seeking employment. The city's population surged, and the demand for housing became acute. This wartime pressure further fueled high-density urban development, setting the stage for the post-war housing boom.

This intense period of state-led development and wartime mobilization left Castejón with a large industrial capacity and a rapidly growing population, but also with a severe housing shortage. This dual legacy directly catalyzed the large-scale urban redevelopment projects and the construction of the first residential towers, known as the "[[W:Tower block|multo altos]]," that would define the beginning of the "[[Baliscan economic miracle]]" in the 1950s. Around this time, the 50-metre height restriction, imposed on all buildings since 1924, was relaxed due to the increased demand for office buildings and advancements in [[W:Earthquake-resistant structures|earthquake-proof construction]]. Starting with the [[Empúries Bank Building]] (312 metres) in 1953, skyscrapers began to dominate Castejón's skyline. During this period of rapid construction, the population of the Metropolis surpassed 12 million people, and various "[[Planned communities in Balisca|new town]]" housing development projects were built in the late 1960s and 1970s to house migrant communities on the outskirts of the Metropolis.

From the 1950s onwards, Castejón's [[Castejón Metro|Metro]] and [[Transport in the Alpujarra|railway network]] saw significant expansion, culminating in the launch of the Algarves' first dedicated [[W:High-speed rail|high-speed railway]] line, the ''[[Porciúncula–Jatiel high-speed rail line|Direto]]'', between [[Macaio]] and [[Jatiel]] in 1969. 
==== Annexation of Trinacria ====
{{See also|1974 Trinacria referendum}}
[[File:Trinacia in Castejon (transparent).svg|right|frameless|The territory of Trinacria in red. Its annexation increased the land area of the Metropolis by a third.]]
Following lengthy negotiations, the land that [[Trinacria|later became Trinacria]] was annexed by the Metropolitan City in line with the results of the [[1975 Trinacria referendum]], with 68% in favor on a turnout of 46%. The Metropolis additionally approved the payment of $225 million (around $1.1 billion in 2021) to the affected Araucarian and Bayaran regions that granted the Metropolis the right to construct and subsequently maintain {{W|water reservoir}}s within their territory for fifty years (1975-2025). The political controversy surrounding the construction and acquisition of these reservoirs set the precedent for grassroots opposition to further "pipelining" expansion of the Metropolis, and signaled the beginning of a new era of local activism in the Alpujarra, by misappropriating environmental protections for [[W:NIMBY|NIMBY]] political purposes.

The new borough's ambitious development goals required an unprecedentedly large workforce, which also sparked a population boom in communes bordering the Metropolis, transforming them into {{W|commuter town}}s. Amongst these was [[Amposta]], [[León]] and [[Yerba Buena]]. This was aided by the expansion of [[AVB]] service in the [[Alpujarra]]. In the late-1970s, grassroots groups and the cooperatives of the [[Baliscan Railways Group]] worked to increase cantonal efforts to prevent further expansion of [[W:Car dependency|car-centric infrastructure]] with long term development of commuter links being central to the goals of Trinacria. This coalition strengthened to continuously support the development of new [[W:Mixed-use developments|mixed-use]] [[W:New town|new towns]] [[W:Transit-oriented development|alongside]] their existing and new routes and stations and adjust their train schedules in order to provide existing and prospective commuters with convenient work-commute routines.
[[File:1982 earthquake damage.png|left|thumb|A partially collapsed tower underconstruction in [[Santa Catalina]] ([[Trinacria]]). Many structures collapsed during the [[1982 Tava Morotí earthquake|earthquake]] as a result of {{W|soil liquefaction}}.]]
Development was severely hampered following the [[1982 Tava Morotí earthquake]], with resources having to be diverted to help with recovery operations. The project was again hampered by [[North Ethiopian tropical cyclone]] [[Hurricane Amara|Amara]] that next year, a rare storm in the [[Northern Hemisphere]] which battered the Concordia conurbation. 

Primarily populated by individuals unable to afford home prices in the more dense boroughs across Salto Bay, Trinacria quickly developed a radically localist {{W|YIMBY}} mindset towards development, especially towards [[W:Affordable housing|affordable housing]] for migrants, transit,  and opposition to private car usage. However, [[W:NIMBY|NIMBYism]] (known locally as SPAN; ''Sí, pero aqui no -'' "Yes, But Not Here") from a small number of groups in Trinacria, including several members of the local development council. This led to delays regarding new housing construction, transit expansion, bike infrastructure as the disputes underwent [[Reconciliation council (Balisca)|reconciliation efforts]]. NIMBYs often called for restrictions on buildings' shadows, concerns over car traffic, parking and crime as reasons for opposing projects. Housing cooperatives, and migration advocates argued that new housing construction would make housing more affordable, thereby pre-empting displacement, personal financial burdens, and rejected the expansion of car infrastructure into the new borough. Thanks to {{W|grassroots activism}}, YIMBY arguments ultimately prevailed.

Communal efforts promoting the borough with its subsidized housing prices and {{W|public transit}} accessibility picked up in the late 1980s. In 1989, the Castejón Housing Authority waitlist had reached over 312,000 units. The [[Housing authority (Balisca)|Housing Authority]], in partnership with dozens of real estate cooperatives, and with the backing of the Baliscan Secretariat of Development, embarked on the [[Millón Más de Programes|Programes Un Millón Más]] (PUM; "one million more program"), an ambitious plan to build enough housing units to accommodate a million new residents. Land acquisition for the project was controversial, with the Castejón government having to resort to the process of "[[communal acquisition]]" in protracted legal battles in an attempt to overcome NIMBY delay tactics.
[[File:Alagón-Ampurias Docklands 1998.jpg|thumb|The Metropolis recorded a population of 17 million at the beginning of the 1990s, in the face of an emerging [[Baliscan housing shortage|housing shortage]]]]
The Authority and its partners initially opted to revive the practice of constructing hundreds of mid and high-rise apartment blocks, popularly called ''multo altos'' throughout the city. The blocks were varying in design and height, but were all expected to meet certain requirements: to comfortably house at least 500 people, have laundromats, recreation centers/parks, water, waste management, and electrical services and earthquake shelters. By 1995, the Castejón Housing Authority, increasingly unpopular and saddled with lawsuits, was dissolved and replaced with smaller, [[Borough Development Board (Castejón)|borough-level cooperatives]]. From that point, these decentralized cooperatives were largely in charge of the program, and shifted in favor of {{W|mixed-use development}}s that were grouped together to form their own self-contained communities. The communities found substantial success, and was soon replicated in cities across the Confederation.

PUM surpassed its goal in October 1997, and with much fanfare was declared completed by Metropolitan Council at a public ceremony together with the worker cooperatives involved in the development. The city's population at the time of the project’s completion had surpassed eleven million and was continuing to increase.[[File:Trinacria housing estate.png|thumb|285x285px|The {{W|mixed-use development|mixed-use}} {{W|transit village}} of [[Berreyarza]] (Trinacria) is one of the largest [[Planned communities in Balisca|p]]<nowiki/>[[Planned communities in Balisca|lanned communities]] in the Metropolis]]
===Recent===
[[File:Bapo hurricane castejon.png|left|thumb|Flooding in [[Coruxón]] in midst of sulcarano Bapo.]]
On 2 February 2015, [[W:South Atlantic tropical cyclone#Subtropical%20Storm%20Bapo|tropical storm Bapo]] formed in the North Ethiopic. Initially forecast to stay offshore and to only be a weak tropical storm at peak intensity, Bapo instead underwent [[W:Explosive intensification|explosive intensification]] to reach peak winds of 165 mph (270 km/h), the equivalent of a Category 4 hurricane on the [[W:SSHWS|SSHWS]]. It made landfall on 5 February at peak intensity, causing massive flooding along with land and mudslides across the [[Salto Bay]] and low-lying parts of the surrounding regions. Once inland, the hurricane quickly weakened before dissipating the following day.  

Most damage and fatalities were caused by high winds and the destructive storm surge to coastal areas of the Concordia area, flooding numerous streets, tunnels, and subway lines (most notably in [[Mercadero]]). It flooded numerous other low-lying areas of [[Ampurias]], [[Expansión]], and [[Limón]]. Electrical power was lost in many parts of the city and its suburbs. It was the most powerful storm to hit Balisca since [[Hurricane Amara|sulcarano Amara]] in 1983. A shelter-in-place order was issued for several barrios surrounding [[Sant Dídac]] after several cranes were toppled by winds and glass panels started being blown off the side of the buildings. It was widely reported that trees had been uprooted throughout the city and power lines had been downed by the storm, temporarily leaving an estimated four million without power. 

The city went into lockdown on March 2020 amidst the first wave of the {{W|COVID-19 pandemic}}. As of December 2021, Castejón has experienced the most deaths of any locality in the coronavirus pandemic in [[Concordia]], which itself has the highest number of confirmed coronavirus cases of any area in Balisca. During the first wave, one-third of total known Balisca cases were in the Castejón Metropolis.
{{Clear}}

