In 2025, the metropolitan [[W:Gross domestic product|gross domestic product]] amounted to ε1.482 trillion with a GDP per capita (PPP) amounting to ε60,543, making it the most powerful region by gross GDP in Abyala, and the [[List of cities by GDP|world's largest urban agglomeration economy]]. The greater [[Concordia]] conurbation (which consists of adjacent parts of the neighboring [[Communities of Balisca|communities]]) had a GDP amounting to ε2,021 billion (equivalent to ε55,815 in per capita terms) in 2024. 

The economy of the Metropolis is based largely on small and medium-sized [[W:Cooperative|cooperatives]] dealing with services and commerce; of the 190,480 cooperatives or enterprises in the city, 80.6 percent are engaged in commerce, transportation, and diverse services, 6.5 percent in construction, and just 3.8 percent in industry. At the 2020 census, 59.5% of occupations in the metropolis were in market services (12.0% in wholesale and retail trade, 9.7% in professional, scientific, and technical services, 6.5% in information and communication, 6.5% in transportation and warehousing, 5.9% in finance and insurance, 5.8% in administrative and support services, 4.6% in accommodation and food services, and 8.5% in various other market services), 26.9% in non-market services (10.4% in human health and social work activities, 9.6% in public administration and defense, and 6.9% in education), 8.2% in manufacturing and utilities (6.6% in manufacturing and 1.5% in utilities), 5.2% in construction, and 0.2% in agriculture.

It has a "very high" rating on the {{W|List of countries by inequality-adjusted Human Development Index|inequality-adjusted Human Development Index}}, earning a score of .849 in 2025, the highest score for a sub-national division in the Baliscan Confederation, and the highest in any sub-national division in Abyala. Additionally, the 2023 [[Social Development Index]] determined Castejón to be the most socially developed region of Balisca. The Metropolis has the highest {{W|living wage}} (per hour) in the Confederation, which was most recently raised to S/38.44 (ε30.75) in 2024. However, incomes are typically much higher due to {{W|reservation wage}}s supported through {{W|collective bargaining}} power by [[trade unions in Balisca|trade unions]]. Castejón overall has an average poverty rate of 4.2 percent, which is above the national rate of 1.67 percent. 
[[File:Ampurias Tower from Tistaina Canal.png|left|thumb|300x300px|The skyline of [[Ampurias]], Castejón's primary [[W:Central business district|business district]]. The Metropolis has witnessed a boom in construction of {{W|high rise building|high rise}} buildings since the beginning of the 2000s]]
Notable Baliscan enterprises based in the city include the [[Luna Corporation]], [[Almercado Co.]], [[León Inc.]], [[Baliscana|Baliscan Airlines]], the [[Baliscan Railways Group]], [[Castejón Communications]] (Castel), the [[Castejón Broadcasting Collective]], and the [[Baliscan Aerospace Corporation]].  

With 16% of its area covered by forest, Castejón has extensive growths of {{W|Nothofagus dombeyi|coigüe}} and {{W|fitzroya|lahuán}}, especially in the mountainous northern and southern boroughs of [[Calañas]] and [[Trinacria]], respectfully. Trinacria contains substantial old growth {{W|Eucalyptus regnans|mountain ash}}. Decreases in the price of timber, increases in the cost of production, and growing opposition from environmentalists have resulted in the complete elimination of Castejón's output. In addition, [[W:Pollen|pollen]], is a major allergen for the nearby population centers. Salto Bay was once a major source of fish, but the practice of commercial fishing was successfully banned via a 1992 referendum after years of advocacy by the [[Ecological Action|Alpujarras Movement]]. Since then, most of Castejón's fish consumption comes from {{W|fish farming|artificial sources}}.

Tourism has become a significant contributor to the metropolitan economy over the last two decades. In 2019, 13.6 million international and 420 million domestic visits to Castejón were made; the economic value of these visits totaled ε90 billion (around 130 billion sol) according to the Metropolitan Government. Many tourists visit the various downtowns, stores, and entertainment districts throughout the neighborhoods of the Metropolis' boroughs. Cultural offerings include both omnipresent Baliscan pop culture, {{W|drug tourism|drug}} and {{W|sex tourism}} and associated districts such as [[Navajas]] and [[Capitoline Hill]], subcultural attractions such as [[Newseum]] center, as well as museums like the [[Cooperative Museum of Castejón]] and the [[Museum of Pre-Columbian History|Museum of Indigenous History]].

===Social protection and mobility===
{{See also|Social protection in Balisca|Baliscan Social Living Index}}
[[File:El Capitan housing estates.png|thumb|[[W:Housing cooperative|Housing co-ops]] in [[El Capitán]]'s [[Cheneración LXXIII]] barrio, some of which are amongst the largest housing cooperatives in the country]]
Castejón has a broad-reaching {{W|social protection}} system based on the [[Baliscan values|Baliscan model]], which ensures that all of Castejón's residents receive tax-funded {{W|social care}}. Expenses to vital medicine is fully funded, and some non-vital medical treatments can potentially be funded, through Castejón's [[W:Collaborative e-democracy|collaboratively-developed]] [[Social Card|social card]] (c''arta social''). Utilizing the [[communal key]] technology, the social card also automatically registers with the [[Cosmo Network|Cosmo co-op system]], allowing the individual access to a variety of communal and cantonal cooperatives, including: [[W:Community garden|community gardens]], [[W:Work sharing|work sharing]], [[W:Self-managed social centers|self-managed social centers]], [[W:cooperative learning|learning co-ops]]<nowiki/>and [[W:Anarchism and education|schools]], transit information, and {{W|childcare service|childcare cooperatives}}. In 2022, 92.4% of the metropolitan population participates in at least one of the co-ops integrated with the social card. All residents of Castejón above 18 years of age can apply for confederal-funded [[social protection in Balisca|social protection]] if they cannot sustain themselves or their family.

Having been developed extensively over the last ten years, approval for the communal social card is almost automatic, and once approved the individual is immediately registered to their communal voting roll. For those that are severely sick, they will receive some financial support throughout the extent of their illness and not just for the maximum of 1 year as previously. Their ability to work are usually re-evaluated by the municipality after 12 months of illness. 

Castejón has the highest pensions in Balisca, and some of the highest in Abyala. The lowest-income group before retirement from the age of 65 receive 80% of their pre-retirement income in pension and miscellaneous subsidies.

==Administration and politics==
{{See also|Confederal government of Balisca|Politics of Balisca}}
[[File:Centre Cívico.jpg|thumb|361x361px|The [[Centre Cívico]] barrio ([[Sástago]]) is the seat of the metropolitan administration and houses the [[Metropolitan Council of Castejón|Metropolitan Council]] building (right)]]
In 1836, the [[District of Castejón]] was founded, consisting of the surrounding rural and mountainous areas to secure the strategic mountain passes to the north and northwest to protect the city in event of an [[W:Insurrection|insurrection]] or [[W:Invasion|invasion]] (the 1826 [[Siege of Castejón]] was nearly successful), and it expanded progressively over the 19th and 20th centuries. The last changes to the limits of Castejón were made in 1975, bringing the area to the current 7,950 km<sup>2</sup> (3,070 sq mi) by incorporating the borough of [[Trinacria]]. At the same time the total number of communes within Castejón reached 705, divided into 121 [[Commune district|commune districts]]. 

In response to militant demands for autonomy during the [[Cantonal War|cantonal rebellions]], Castejón received a greater degree of autonomy with the 1883 elaboration of the first Statute of Government (Estatuto de Gobierno) and the creation of an assembly of representatives, the [[Metropolitan Council of Castejón|Metropolitan Council]]. As part of these, the budget was administered locally; it was proposed by the head of council and approved by the Metropolitan Council. However, the [[Confederal Congress]] continued to set the ceiling to internal and external public debt issued by the city government.
[[File:MetCo hall.png|left|thumb|The hall of the [[Metropolitan Council of Castejón]]]]
On 11 October 1920, Castejón ceased to be the Confederal District (''Distrito Confederal'' or D.C.) entirely, and was officially renamed ''Ciudad Metropolitana de Castejón'' (or "CDMC"). On that date, Castejón completed it's transition into the confederation's sixth confederal-level entity, giving it a level of autonomy on par to that of the reformed [[Communities of Balisca|communities]]. The [[W:Municipal council|regional council]], the Metropolitan Council of Castejón (commonly referred to as the ''MetCo'') now performs all administrative roles within the metropolis. 

Citizens have the power to remove any council member from office through {{W|recall election}}s, supported by at least fifteen percent of the electorate. The Metropolitan Council operates on a {{W|Participatory democracy|participatory}} and {{W|Consensus democracy|consensus-based approach}}, and is responsible for passing laws and budgets, though ''Castejónes'' also make extensive use of direct [[W:Popular initiatives|popular initiatives]] to pass or amend legislation. The members of the MetCo are elected as representatives of specific [[commune district]]<nowiki/>s within the metropolis. Upon the death, recall or resignation of a metropolitan delegate, a special election is called in their [[commune district]].

The politics pursued by the Metropolis since the end of the 19th century have been more [[W:Permissive society|permissive]] than those of the rest of the country, whether with the support of the [[Confederal government of Balisca|confederal administration]] or not. This can be attributed to Castejón's outsized economic, social and political sway in the confederation as a result of decades of [[W:Centralism (Peru)|centralism]]. Castejón is considered one of the most socially progressive cities in the world, and is considered to be representative of contemporary [[Baliscan values]] internationally. It was amongst the earliest adopters of legal {{W|cannabis (drug)|cannabis}} and {{W|hemp}} (1904), the first to protect {{W|same-sex marriage}} (in 1948), first to legalize [[Prostitution in Balisca|prostitution]] (1888), first to ban {{W|conversion therapy}} and the {{W|gay panic defense}} (both 1963) and has arguably the most developed [[Social protection in Balisca|social protection]] system in the Baliscan Confederation. Additionally, since the 1970s, the city's residential voting population has become increasingly cosmopolitan, and is aligned against [[W:Sectarianism|sectarianism]] and [[W:Xenophobia|xenophobia]], more so than it has ever been in the past. In the [[2021 Baliscan confederal election]], cosmopolitan political factions received more than 90% of the vote across Castejón's commune districts, a level of support that had not been reached prior. 

