As of the [[2025 Baliscan census]], the Metropolis ([[Regions of Balisca|region]]) had a population of 24,479,594 people. The Metropolis subsequently has a population of around {{convert|2900|PD/sqkm|PD/sqmi}}, a ratio of men to women of 0.98:1, with 99.68% of the population defined as urban. The population is highly decentralized, being well dispersed across the Metropolis' twelve boroughs. Castejón is considered to be the [[W:primate city|primate city]] of Balisca, being it's most populous city by a significant margin, being twice as populous as [[Macaio]], the next most-populous city. 

The first census in the area was carried out in 1840 during the [[Anyos Federales|Reconstruction]] era, and recorded a population of 349,278, with the majority of these being of free people of [[Kafto-Baliscans|Kaftian]] descent. Castejón's population increased significantly between 1850 and 1920, and again between 1930 and 1990. Population growth (including immigration) slowed as a result of the [[Special Period]] and the [[Camboriú War]], although this rapidly rebounded by the end of the 1970s, driven largely by the rise of [[W:Tech startup|tech startups]] that attracted potential workers and aspiring students.

The proportion of people under 15 is 29.1%, above the Baliscan average of 27.6%, and the proportion of people 65 and older is relatively low, at 8.1%. Castejón is slightly below the average confederal population growth rate, recently about 1.0% a year, as well as a comparatively low infant mortality rate. The Metropolis' {{W|total fertility rate}} of 1.66 children per woman is slightly lower than the Baliscan average (1.71 children per woman as of 2025), and at the {{W|sub-replacement fertility}} level. The median age is approximately 36.08 years and life expectancy at birth is 83.5 years. This low birthrate is contrasted by its high rate of incoming migrants, estimated at 10 immigrants per 1,000 residents per year.
===Ethnography===
{{See also|Ethnography of Balisca|Immigration to Balisca}}There have been numerous migrations towards Castejón since the end of the 18th century, when the city emerged as a popular destination for [[W:Political refugees|political refugees]] from neighboring or more distant countries, particularly Brazil. The city has historically been a major [[W:Port of entry|port of entry]] for immigrants to Balisca. Attracted by the industrial opportunities, many workers moved in, first from the other Baliscan regions (mainly rural residents from [[Araucaria]] and [[Bayara]]), then from Southern Europe and [[W:North Africa|North Africa]], and more recently from across [[Sudanese Kaftia]], along with [[North Fosia|North]] and [[Northeast Fosia|Northeast Fosian]] countries.

Nowadays, Castejón is home to a diverse group of immigrant communities, with no one country or region dominating. Since Balisca's embrace of [[W:Free migration|free migration]] policies in the post-revolution period, the proportion of families in Castejón that can claim at least one foreign ancestor has grown significantly. In the most recent Baliscan census (in 2025), 66% of inhabitants in the Metropolis answered that they were [[Baliscan nationality law|Baliscan citizens]], born as such in Balisca, indicating that a third of residents had not been born in the country. At the same time, the [[Instituto Confederal de Stadística]] (ICS), taking into account the nationality of birth of the parents, found that 40.1% of the population of the Metropolis was of foreign origin. Among residents aged under 18, 53.3% were of foreign origin.

This large concentration of immigrants and their descendants includes many of [[Central Kaftia|Central Kaftian]] (mainly [[W:Riffian|Riffian]] and other [[Imfezzan Baliscans|Imfezzans]]) and [[North Kaftia|North Kaftian]] ancestry, together with [[Volisanian-speaking Kaftian countries|Volisanian-speaking Black Kaftians]] from [[Bonande]] and [[tba]]. Many immigrants were naturalised following the 1997 liberalization of naturalisation process. In 2020, about 12% of city residents were of non-Baliscan Abyalan origin (mainly expatriates from countries like [[Asikyira]], [[Lacashe]], [[Potocsí]], [[Balboa]], and [[tba]]) and 24% were of another background, mostly from the [[Levant]], [[East Fosia]], and [[Sudanese Kaftia]]. Among all major migrant groups from outside Abyala, a majority of the permanent residents have acquired Baliscan nationality.

===Education===
[[File:Capitoline Hill.png|thumb|250x250px|The campus of the {{W|research university|research}}-focused [[University of Castejón, Capitoline]] (UCC). It is a [[cooperative university]] and is one of the highest performing universities in Balisca.]]
[[File:Concordia University campus.png|thumb|250px|The campus of [[Concordia University]], another [[cooperative university]] located in Castejón's [[Trinacria]] borough.]]{{Further|Education in Balisca}}
The educational system is divided into {{W|preschool}} (for those under age 6), basic education (9 years, in three stages, compulsory), {{W|secondary education}} (3 years, compulsory since 1950), and higher education (subdivided in {{W|university}} and {{W|polytechnic}} education). Universities are usually organized into faculties. Institutes and schools are also common designations for autonomous subdivisions of higher education institutions. Education is based on {{W|cooperative learning}} and the {{W|Montessori education|Montessori method of education}}, which seeks to develop natural interests and activities rather than use formal teaching methods. During their nine years of common basic education, students are not selected, tracked, or streamed. There is also inclusive special education within the classroom and instructional efforts to minimize low achievement. 

The Castejón Metropolis is home to a large number of colleges and universities. The three most competitive universities are Concordia University, [[University of Castejón, Capitoline]], and Southern University, all considered among the most highly performing universities in the Southern Hemisphere. Many scholars have pointed out the overlap of education and the economy within the Concordia Area. According to multiple studies, [[Cooperative university|cooperative universities]], such as [[Sérregio University]], University of Castejón - Capitoline and the [[Cosmopolitan University]] significantly contribute to the regional [[W:Cultural mosaic|cultural mosaic]]. These universities also provide numerous {{W|cooperative education}} programs for people to learn and enhance skills relevant to their own communities. 

Some of the largest and most prestigious universities in Castejón are:

{{Div col}}
* [[University of Castejón]]
* [[Metropolitan University of Castejón]]
* [[Hesperides College of the Arts]]
* [[Concordia University]]
* [[University of Southern Balisca]]
* [[Muikuo University]]
* [[Cosmopolitan University]]
* [[Southern University]] ([[COCO]])
* [[Castejón University of Technology and Design]]
* [[Balisca Advanced Institute of Science & Technology]]
* [[Basilio Institute of Technology]]
* [[Baliscan Institute of the Arts]]
{{Div col end}}

Total adult literacy rate is 100 percent in Castejón. Primary and secondary school enrollments are also both 100 percent, while 63% of college-age students choose to attend a higher education institution, higher than the 55% rate seen nationally, and the 35% average rate in the OECD countries. Public education is fully funded by the [[regions of Balisca|regions]], and thus there are no tuition fees levied on students who attend public learning institutions. All students attend a cooperatively of publicly-run institution of learning. {{W|Religious schools}}, {{W|private schools}} and {{W|homeschooling}} are constitutionally prohibited in the Metropolis.

Castejón is also an education hub, with more than 80,000 international students in 2006. University admission is highly competitive and selective in Castejón. Long-term residents of Castejón often find themselves having to compete with prospective students from across the Baliscan Confederation and international students for the few available admission slots at one of Castejón's # universities.

In 2023, 25% of all students in Castejón's universities were international students—the maximum cap allowed, a majority from [[East Fosia]], [[Sudanese Kaftia]] and [[North Fosia]]. Boosted by the spread of the internet, the [[Metropolitan Council for Knowledge Sharing]] (CCC) has pioneered methods such as [[W:Distance education|distance education]] and [[W: Out-of-school learning|out-of-school learning]] to overcome this limit. In regards to [[Baliscan social internationalism]], the CCC is currently replicating these methods as part of a push to expand access to education in the [[periphery countries]].

Castejón's students have excelled in many of the world education benchmarks in maths, science and reading. In 2015, both its primary and secondary students rank highly in [[AMDC]] global school performance rankings across 76 countries—described as the most comprehensive map of education standards. In 2016, Castejón's students ranked highly in both the [[W:Program International Student Assessment|Program International Student Assessment]] (PISA) and the [[W:Trends in International Mathematics and Science Study|Trends in International Mathematics and Science Study]] (TIMSS).

