===Sexual orientation and gender identity===
{{Main|LGBTQ rights in Balisca}}

{{See|LGBTQ history in Balisca|Marriage in Balisca}}
[[File:Bathers at San Niccolo.jpg|left|thumb|260x260px|[[W:Hammam|''Hamán'']] ''en Castejón,'' {{Circa|1670}} provides a glimpse into the long history of [[W:Non-heterosexual|non-heteronormative]] and [[W:Homoeroticism|homoerotic]] culture in the city, dating back to the 17th century.]]
The Castejón Metropolis is home to a prominent self-identifying {{W|LGBTQ}} community estimated at approximately 4,969,033 individuals in 2024, the largest in Balisca, the largest in the Algarves, and one of the world's largest. Castejón also is home to the largest [[W:Transgender#Population figures and prevalence|transgender population]] within Balisca, some 200,000 individuals. In line with prevailing [[Baliscan values]], Castejón has long protected the right of consenting adults to form whatever [[Marriage in Balisca|familial arrangements]] they choose, including [[W:Polyamory|polyamorous relationship]] and {{W|group marriage}}, as long as all parties involved willingly consented (informed consent) and have equal rights within the relationship. This approach emphasizes the importance of {{W|personal autonomy}} and the explicit right to non-interference from the state in matters of personal relationships.
[[File:Barrio X.png|thumb|297x297px|Barrio X in the [[Papallona]] district ([[Expansión]]) was the original "[[W:gay village|queer village]]" of Castejón]]
Castejón's LGBTQ culture has its roots in the city's own origin in the 17th century as a frontier settlement outside of colonial control, what has been called a "history of [[W:sexual liberation|sexual permissiveness]] and its function as a wide-open settlement – a place where anything goes". The advent of the [[Volisanya Boba|''Volisanya Boba'']] period saw a boom in population from 5,000 to 85,000 residents between 1710 and 1810. These migrants were composed of traders and fortune seekers from a variety of nationalities and cultures, over 95% of whom were young men.

These transient and diverse populations thrust into a relatively anarchic environment were less likely to conform to [[W:Social conventions|social conventions]]. For example, with an unbalanced gender ratio, men often assumed roles [[W:Gender roles|conventionally assigned]] to women in social and domestic settings. [[W:Cross dressing|Cross-gender dress]] and same-sex dancing were prevalent at [[W:Masquerade ball|masquerade balls]] where men assumed the traditional role of women going so far as to wear female attire. There is well documentation of numerous cases of women who donned men's clothing in public spaces for increased social and economic freedom, safety, and gender-progressive experimentation. [[W:Queer fashion|Queer]] and [[W:genderless fashion in Japan|genderless fashion]] is still an important part of LGBTQ culture in the city today.

Following the consolidation of Castejón in 1902, the metropolis' LGBTQ population has been concentrated around the [[Papallona]] barrio in the [[Expansión]] borough. Papallona has become renowned its acceptance of {{W|gender diversity}}, and locally owned businesses, including restaurants, cafés, bars, clubs, trendy thrift-stores, and other independent specialty stores. Papallona has a high population density compared to many other neighborhoods in Castejón, and it has a politically active LGBTQ community. The metropolis' large LGBTQ population has established and sustained a politically and culturally active community for well over a century, developing a powerful presence in Castejón civic life. Survey data released in 2020 by [[Instituto Confederal de Stadística]] (ICS) places the proportion of LGBTQ adults in the Metropolis at 21.3%, which is the second-highest proportion (after [[Macaio]]) of the 20 most populous metropolitan areas as measured by the polling organization.
[[File:Tistaina Canal Pride.png|thumb|310x310px|2025 [[W:Critical pride|Critical Pride]] Parade on the [[Tistaina Canal]]]]
The {{W|red-light district}}s of the [[Llanito]] and [[Limón]] boroughs are also closely related to the development of modern LGBTQ culture in Balisca. Its history with sexuality began with the development of {{W|sex industry|adult entertainment}} in the middle of the 19th century. In the middle of the 20th century, as the number of {{W|massage parlors}}, {{W|brothels}}, nightclubs and {{W|gay bars}} increased, Limón became the most famous spot for {{W|sex tourism}}, eventually growing into a major attraction for clients from across the world.

The annual Castejón Pride March (or queer [[W:Pride parade|pride parade]]) traverses southeastward from [[Plaza de Flores y Amor]] in Papallona and ends at [[La Portapí|Portopí]] in [[Ampurias]]; the parade has grown to become the largest pride parade in the world. In June 2023, the parade attracted tens of thousands of participants and 6 million sidewalk spectators. The Castejón Pride movement has maintained its relatively militant character, resisting co-optation and the influence of {{W|pink money}} and {{W|rainbow capitalism}}.

==Environment and geography==
{{Main|Geography of Balisca}}

Castejón is located in the southern region of the island of [[Atlántida]], centered on the [[Alpujarra]], surrounded by the [[Matarrañya|Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]]. The metropolis surrounds [[Salto Bay]], which connects the city to the [[Guadalquivir]] and the wider [[Aethiopian Ocean#Northern Ethiopic|Northern Aethiopian Ocean]]. The peaks surrounding Castejón are always covered with snow year-round. 18% of the city is reserved as green spaces, and in 2012 the city passed legislation requiring all new construction over two stories in height to have [[W:rooftop garden|rooftop]] or [[W:Vertical gardens|vertical gardens]] (or dedicated green space) in an effort to combat pollution that becomes a significant problem in the winter. Following a [[1982 Tava Morotí earthquake|devastating earthquake in 1982]], the city [[W:Freeway removal|demolished]] its remaining [[W:Elevated highway|elevated sections of highway]], opting to move them underground and redeveloping the vacant areas into green spaces or mixed-use development.   

The city made solar panels for {{W|solar heating}} of water mandatory in 1992.  
[[File:Alexander Avenue Bridge with Mt. Tamalpais.jpg|thumb|[[Mount Panteón]], the highest point in the Castejón Metropolis]]
There are more than 200 hills within the limits of the metropolis. Numerous neighborhoods are named after the hill on which they are situated, including [[Capitoline Hill]], [[Cantonese Hill]], [[Mokõi-sy]] and [[Apyká-sy]]. Near the geographic center of the city, southwest of the downtown area, are a series of less densely populated hills. [[Moorish Hill|Moorish Point]], a hill forming one of the city's highest points, forms an overlook spot over [[Trinacria]]. Castejón's tallest point, [[Mount Panteón|Panteón]], is {{convert|1898|m|ft|0}} high and is topped with an active archaeological site related to the [[Kuarupíba|Kuarupíba indigenous culture]].

===Climate===
[[File:Trinacria fog.png|left|thumb|300x300px|Fog rolling in over [[Moorish Hill]] in[[Trinacria]], which is characteristic of the city in the summer.]]
Castejón has a temperate climate, classified in the {{W|Mediterranean climate|Mediterranean}} zone by the main climatic classification ([[W:Köppen climate classification|Köppen]]: ''Csb'') but some sources put the city in the {{W|oceanic climate|oceanic zone}} (Cfb). The climate is sometimes characterized as a "modified Mediterranean" climate because it is cooler and wetter than a "true" Mediterranean climate, but shares the characteristic dry summer (which has a strong influence on the region's vegetation). It has cool Winters (June to August), warm-hot Summers (December to February), mild Autumns (March to May) and volatile Springs (September to November); The climate is characterized for having mild temperatures, without harsh cold or extreme heat, thanks to the effects of the [[Ethiopian Ocean#Northern Ethiopic|North Aethiopic]], the Guadalquivir and [[Salto Bay]]. There are numerous thunderstorms, and although rare, has experienced [[W:Extratropical cyclone|extratropical cyclone]]. Rainfall is regular and evenly spread throughout the year, reaching around 950 millimeters (37 in).

Castejón has the lowest daily mean, maximum, and minimum temperatures in June, July, and August. During the summer, rising hot air in the [[Levante, Balisca|Levante's]] interior valleys creates a low pressure area that draws winds from the ocean through the [[Guadalquivir]], which creates the city's characteristic cool winds and fog. This fog has affectionately been named ''Nolo'' by locals; a play on the [[Baliscano]] word ''nólo'', which can be translated 'I will not' or 'I refuse' — evoking a kind of moody refusal to leave that is characteristic of fog. ''Nolo'' is less pronounced in western neighborhoods and during the late summer and early fall.

Because of its varied topography and maritime influences, Castejón exhibits a multitude of distinct {{W|microclimate}}s. The high hills in the geographic east of the city are responsible for a 20% variance in annual rainfall between different parts of the city. They also protect neighborhoods directly to their west from the foggy and sometimes very cold and windy conditions experienced in Ampurias; for those who live on the western side of the city, Castejón is sunnier, with an average of 260 clear days, and only 105 cloudy days per year.

Summers are warm-hot and humid, with less wind than other seasons. The average temperature in this season is {{convert|23|C|F}}. Daytime temperatures are usually between {{convert|24|C|F}} and {{convert|32|C|F}}, while night lows between {{convert|14|C|F}} and {{convert|22|C|F}}. During this season, a moderate wind often blows into [[Alpujarra]] from the sea in the evenings which has a pleasant cooling effect on the city, in contrast to the more severe summer heat of nearby cities like [[Valparaíso]]. Heat waves come with the western winds, which bring humid and hot air masses from the tropical interior of [[Atlántida]]; temperatures can rise above {{convert|35|C|F}}. These warm periods are usually followed by stronger {{W|thunderstorm}}s, generated by cold fronts from the southeast that lowers temperatures considerably. This phenomenon is regional, and can occur several times all year long.

Average annual water surface temperatures are between {{convert|13|C|F}} on the [[Levante, Balisca|Aethiopic Seaboard]] (similar to western Mesogean waters, such as [[Ampuria]] or [[Tartessia]]), and {{convert|17|C|F}} in [[Salto Bay]] (similar to Northern Mesogean temperatures, such as [[Mazar]] or [[Kankadadka]]). 
