==== Climate change ====
As a coastal city, Castejón will be heavily affected by {{W|climate change}}. As of 2021, sea levels are projected to rise by as much as 5 feet (1.5 m), resulting in periodic flooding, rising groundwater levels, and lowland (such as [[Llanito]] and [[Ampurias]]) floods from more severe storms.

Moreover, according to the 2022 [[W:IPCC Sixth Assessment Report|IPCC Sixth Assessment Report]], Castejón is one of 12 major cities in the Algarves which would be the most severely affected by future sea level rise. It estimates it would sustain cumulative damages of 65 billion sol under RCP 4.5 and 300 billion sol for the high-emission scenario RCP 8.5 by the year 2050. Additionally, RCP 8.5 combined with the hypothetical impact from marine ice sheet instability at high levels of warming would involve up to 350 billion sol in damages, while the additional accounting for the "low-probability, high-damage events" may increase aggregate risks to 687 billion sol for the "moderate" RCP4.5, 838 billion sol for RCP8.5 and 1,030 billion sol under the high-end ice sheet instability scenario. Since sea level rise would continue for about 10,000 years under every scenario of climate change, future costs of sea level rise would only increase, especially without [[W:Climate change adaptation|adaptation measures]].

===Natural disasters===
[[File:1989 Trinacria firestorm.jpeg|thumb|Flames approaching residential areas in the [[W:Wildland–urban interface|wildland–urban interface]] during the [[1989 Trinacria firestorm]]]]
As sea levels continue to rise due to global warming, Castejón is increasingly vulnerable to {{W|storm surges}}, which occur when strong winds push seawater onto the land. The city's low-lying areas, such as [[Ampurias]], [[Coruxón]], [[Expansión]], and [[Sástago]] are particularly susceptible to flooding during storms. [[Sulcarano Amara]], which hit the city in 1983, caused widespread flooding and power outages, resulted in significant social and economic disruption. 

While still relatively rare, heatwaves are set to become increasingly common as a result of of {{W|climate change}}, with the most recent being the [[2023 Baliscan heatwave]]. Heatwaves can lead to heat exhaustion, heat stroke, and other health problems, particularly for vulnerable populations such as the elderly, children, and inadequately housed individuals. The city's infrastructure, such as the metro system, can also be affected by extreme heat, leading to service disruptions.

The [[Yawar Sypa]] fault-line is responsible for much earthquake activity, and the fault passes through the metropolis (across [[Salto Bay]]) itself. It caused the destructive earthquakes in [[1924 Alpujarra earthquake|1924]] and [[1982 Tava Morotí earthquake|1982]], which both caused significant damages and high casualties within the Castejón Metropolis. Minor earthquakes occur on a regular basis. The threat of major earthquakes plays a large role in the city's infrastructure development. The city constructed an [[Castejón Auxiliary Water Supply System|auxiliary water supply system]] and has repeatedly upgraded its building codes, requiring retrofits for older buildings and higher engineering standards for new construction.

The earthquakes are colloquially called the Alpujarran manifestation of the "[[Anhangá curse]]", referencing the [[Kurupiran languages|Kurupiran]] name for the Baliscan archipelago, often translated as 'land of spirits'. This name reflects traditional indigenous and religious [[W:Candomblé|Candomblé]] views of the archipelago as the home of spirits of nature that served as the protector of their domains, who "[...] does not kill to devour, but to restore and to avenge those victimized by insatiable hunters". Other examples of the curse across Baliscan include [[W:landslide|landslides]], [[W:Thunderstorm|lightning storms]], [[W:Hurricane|hurricanes]], and floods.

==Cityscape==
===Architecture and urban design===
[[File:Torreón barrio.png|left|thumb|300x300px|Skyline of the [[Torreón]] barrio ([[Ampurias]]) depicting several eras of metropolitan architecture]]
[[File:Expansion colonial villa.png|thumb|300x300px|A preserved [[Colonial Balisca|colonial-era]] villa in [[Expansión]]]]
The architecture of the metropolis is not so much known for defining a particular {{W|architectural style}}; rather, with its interesting and challenging variations in geography, topology reflective of various periods in it's [[History of Castejón|tumultuous history]], Castejón is known worldwide for its particularly eclectic mix of [[w:Victorian architecture|Victorian]], {{W|Art Deco}}, {{W|Neo-Mudéjar}}, {{W|Moorish Revival architecture|Moorish Revival}}, {{W|Italianate architecture|Italianate}}, [[Cosmocazo|Cosmopolitano]] and modern architecture. The building form most closely associated with Castejón in the 21st century is the {{W|skyscraper}}, which has shifted many commercial and residential districts from low-rise to high-rise areas. Additionally, mostly surrounded by mountains, the city is accumulating one of the [[List of tallest buildings in Balisca|largest and most varied collection of skyscrapers in the world]].

Icons of Castejón's architecture include the [[Mission Sant-Maloù Park]], [[People's Park (Embajadores)|People's Park]] and its [[Arco de Humanidad]], the [[Cosmopolitan Temple]], the [[Baliscan Cabildo]], [[Plaza de Flores y Amor]], and the [[Castejón Basilica|Old Castejón Basilica]].
[[File:People's Park, Embajadores.png|left|thumb|300x300px|[[Arco de Humanidad]] at [[People's Park (Embajadores)|People's Park]] ([[Embajadores]], [[Expansión]])]]
[[File:Los Espasios de Artesanos .png|left|thumb|300x300px|[[Los Espasios de Artesanos]], a [[W:Social housing|social housing]] development in [[Coruxón]]]]
After the [[Great Adonian War]] and the subsequent baby boom, Castejón faced a severe housing shortage; most of the housing in the city dated to the 19th century and was in terrible condition. Only a hundred thousand new housing units were constructed between 1946 and 1950. The number rose to 120,000 in 1951 and more than 150,000 in 1956. The committee of public housing for the Metropolis acquired the cheapest land it could buy, often at the edges of the city. In 1961, when land within the city was exhausted, they were authorized to begin buying land in the surrounding suburbs, leading to quarrels with [[Araucaria|Araucarian]] communities.   
[[File:Original_design_of_Barcelona's_city_blocks.svg|thumb|300x300px|[[Coruxón]]'s grid, which has been replicated in other Baliscan cities]]
Much larger buildings began to appear in the mid-1950s. They were built with prefabricated materials and placed in clusters. They were known as MAs, or ''multo altos'' ("very high" or many deep"). A larger type of public housing began to appear in the mid-1950s, known as a ''barre'', because they were more wide than high. They usually had between 500 and 1000 apartments, were built in clusters, and were [[W:Mixed-use development|mixed-use]], integrating [[W:Cooperative|cooperatives]] with multi-story flats, nurseries and co-ops, with a sufficient connection to public transportation. They were welcomed by the families who lived there in the 1950s and early 1960s, but in later years housing demand far outstripped supply as immigration to the Metropolis increased. 

In January 1964 the confederal government intervened, taking over metropolitan housing development in order to fast-track the construction of more housing and transit expansion. A new urban plan for the Metropolis was adopted by the Metropolitan Council in 1965. Higher buildings were permitted, as long as they met both technical and aesthetic standards. The first new tower to be constructed was an apartment building, the ''Torre Constellación'', in [[Coruxón]]. It was twenty-two stories, and 61 meters high, and was completed in 1967. Between 1965 and 1975, about 300 new buildings higher than thirty stories were constructed in Castejón, more than half of them in [[Ampurias]] and [[Expansión]]. Most of them were a little over one hundred meters high; in several clusters of high-rises. 
[[File:Cabildo of Castejón.png|thumb|300x300px|[[Plaza Confederación]] barrio ([[Expansión]]), which consists countless expressions of early [[Cosmocazo|Cosmopolitano]]-influenced architecture.]]
The growing number of skyscrapers appearing on the Castejón skyline provoked resistance from the local population, particularly those in [[Charxes]] and [[Hisbanya]]. In 1975, the [[Metropolitan Council of Castejón|Metropolitan Council]] declared a moratorium on new towers within the city, and in 1977 the City of Castejón was given a new ''Plano Uso Tierra'' (PUT), which imposed a height limit of one hundred meters outside of the "core boroughs" of Castejón and abolished height limits in the core boroughs. The building of skyscrapers intensified within [[Commune of Castejón|Castejón's core]], particularly in [[Ampurias]] and [[Expansión]].

The architecture of the [[Trinacria]] borough has largely been shaped by its relatively recent development. Because of this relatively short existence and other factors, Trinacria's current urban landscape is mostly modern and contemporary architecture, and older buildings are scarce. Originally intended to be mostly low buildings and packed with {{W|Single-family detached home|single family homes}}, confederal intervention and grassroots activism led the borough to have a larger focus on mid-and-high rise residential homes and densification. The metropolis' culture is changing as well due to increased understanding of natural catastrophes, and because of this architecture has had to make dramatic changes since the 1980s. 

The Castejón Metropolis has grown to over 24 million residents in 2025 and the whole city has moved from its once central location to a more southwestern part of the city. Castejón in recent years has been growing at a substantial pace for a city of its size, and as a result it has had to build up in order to get the most out what scarce land it has. Due to Castejón's extremely limited space, the longstanding policy has been to build "up, not out", meaning [[W:Urban density|taller buildings in closer proximity]] to each other rather than [[W:Urban sprawl|sprawl]]. Significant [[W:Underground city|underground complexes]] and [[W:Skyway|skyways]] now criss-cross the more crowded areas of the city in order to make commuting more efficient.

Throughout most of Castejón's history, the height of buildings has been restricted. These restrictions gradually eroded in the post-war period and high rise buildings have become ever more numerous since, particularly in the 21st century. Skyscrapers were once limited to that of the Ampurias borough, but are now found throughout the city. Notable recent tall buildings include [[Ampurias Tower]] (Gran Castejón Tower), the centerpiece of the Castejón WTC complex, [[22 Ampuria]]: the tallest residential building in the city, and the [[Torre Roja]] (nicknamed ''Aguya Espacial'' or "Space Needle") which has set a precedent for other recent high-rise developments built in a similar high-tech style.

===Boroughs and barrios===
{{main|Boroughs of Castejón|Neighborhoods in Castejón}}

[[File:Takamaicán night.png|left|thumb|300x300px|Ezogashima Gate in [[Takamaicán]] ([[Coruxón]]) during [[W:Lunar New Year|Lunar New Year]] festivities.]]
[[File:Poko Yinyu.png|thumb|300x300px|The [[Poko Yinyu]] {{W|ethnic enclave}}, home to the largest concentration of overseas Kwangjuan in the world.]]
The Metropolis is sometimes referred to collectively as the ''Twelve Boroughs'', reflecting the unique connection of the city with the term. There are [[Barrios in Castejón|hundreds of distinct neighborhoods]] throughout the boroughs (called "barrios"), all with a definable history and character. Some of these districts have a previous history as independent municipalities which were integrated into the city during the late 19th century and the first half of the 20th century, such as [[Arrabád]], [[Albayzín]], [[Talará]] and [[Olocau]]. However, other municipalities which are contiguous to the Metropolis (such as [[Valparaíso]], [[León]], and [[Yerba Buena]]) have remained separate communities to this day, but are included in the much larger [[Concordia|Concordia conurbation]]. These are either informal designations, or reflect the names of towns that have been absorbed by sprawl. These barrios are distinguished by their history, culture, architecture, demographics, and geography. 
[[File:Artes island.png|left|thumb|300x300px|[[Artés|Artes]] island, which has been expanded through [[W:Land reclamation|land reclamation]]. ]]
The names of the over 200 neighborhoods are officially defined by the Metropolitan Capital Office of Planning. Neighborhoods can be defined by the boundaries of boroughs, historic districts, neighborhood commissions, civic associations, and business improvement districts (BIDs); sometimes these boundaries overlap. 

The [[Ampurias]] borough is the main financial district, and it's [[Mercadero]] barrio has recently developed into a new financial and commercial hub in the northern portion of the borough. The [[Charxes]] borough is currently undergoing substantial (re)development with numerous {{W|housing estate}}s under construction along its border with [[Araucaria]]. 85% of all units in solely residential developments must be [[Balisca#Income, wealth, and poverty|rent-exempt]]. For {{W|mixed-use developments}}, this percentage can be reduced to as low as 35%.

[[Llanito]] ("little plain"), located in the western edge of the metropolis is historically infamous for its nightlife and cabarets, as well as [[Prostitution in Balisca|prostitution]] and {{W|antisocial behavior}}, but has changed significantly in recent years and due to its central location within [[Concordia]] and has become a minor attraction of Castejón. It currently has a very diverse immigrant community (47.4% of its population was born abroad, ranging from [[Maharlikans]], [[Darasians]], and Ayori, to a more recent Abyalan community, especially from [[Asikyira]]). It is home to many bars, restaurants, and night spots, and boasts the largest [[Bayani Baliscans|Bayani]] population within Balisca.

[[Expansión]], considered a part of "Core Castejón", is one of Castejón's main entertainment and shopping districts, attracting millions of tourists throughout the year. A flourishing artist community has thrived for decades in Expansión and has become more visible throughout all of its neighborhoods, most notably [[Diferestero]]. Hundreds of art studios and galleries are scattered throughout the neighborhood. Several open studios programs are conducted that enable the public to visit artist studios and galleries. In addition, the world renowned [[Pedralbes Museum of Art]] is located in this commune. It is unique amongst its relatively low property prices, and as a result it is overwhelmingly populated by immigrants (along with their descendants) and millennials.

If the boroughs were each independent cities, three of the boroughs ([[Charxes]], [[Expansión]], and [[Llanito]]) would be among the twenty most populous cities in Balisca; these same boroughs are coterminous with the three of the most densely populated communes in Balisca. Each of the boroughs elect 15 members to the [[Metropolitan Council of Castejón|city's Metropolitan Council]].

====Origin====
The "District of Castejón" was established as a {{W|federal district|federally administered}} capital territory following the passage of the [[Relocation Act]] on 9 July 1836. The historical boundaries of the District were made up by the [[Expansión]], [[Ampurias]], [[Sástago]] and [[Hisbanya]] boroughs, now known as the "core boroughs" of Castejón. The city developed in the post-Civil War period as a market and trading city due to its advantageous location on the [[Alagón river|Alagón]]. At the time, the city was facing a large influx of refugees displaced during the fighting of the [[Baliscan Civil War|civil war]]. Under the guidance of the reform minded [[Confederal Congress]], the groundwork for the expansion of Castejón into a {{W|compact city}} on a "{{W|complete streets|complete}} and {{W|human scale|humane}}" scale was gradually expanded, later going on to adopt many of the ideas of {{W|Ildefons Cerdà}}.

Castejón remained a federally administered district until October 1902, when, after a long political campaign and public relations battle throughout the 1890s, according to the new Municipal Charter of "Greater Castejón," the core city of the Castejón was [[consolidation of Castejón|consolidated]] with other cities, towns, and communes of the [[Alpujarra]] surrounding [[Salto Bay]] to form six constituent boroughs. The new merged government became what is now known as the ''[[Castejón Metropolis]]'', or, ambiguously, ''[[Castejón|Castejón Region]]''.

==Infrastructure==
===Transport===
{{Main|Transport in Concordia}}

{{See also|Transport in Balisca}}
[[File:Communi logo.png|thumb|Logo of [[ComMuni]] (popularly known as the ''Communicucu'' or "ComMuni worm"), the [[W:Cooperative federation|cooperative federation]] of {{W|public transport}} operators in the metropolis]]
[[File:Concordia Airport lounge.jpg|thumb|300x300px|The interior of Terminal 1 of [[Concordia International Airport]], the busiest airport in Balisca]]
Castejón is a major rail, highway, and air transport hub, and as a result it has developed a network of complex infrastructure systems. Being the most populous city in Balisca and the heart of the [[Concordia|Concordia megalopolis]], Castejón has a transportation system which includes one of the largest {{W|rapid transit}} systems in the world; the world's first mechanically ventilated {{W|vehicular tunnel}}; and an [[W:Aerial tramway|aerial tramway]]. Castejón is also home to an extensive {{W|bike freeway}} system which services the entire metropolis. Large {{W|bike garage|bicycle storage facilities}} are present throughout the city. Personal cars are significantly less prevalent compared to other cities in the rest of the world, a result of metropolitan and confederal policies that discourage [[W:Car ownership|car ownership]] and [[W:Car dependency|dependency]]. Castejón is one of eight major Baliscan cities in which less than a sixth of households own a car.

According to a 2024 [[Instituto Confederal de Stadística]] (ICS) survey, a large majority of Castejón's residents (59.0 percent) use public transport to get around the metropolis. Only 6.6 percent commuted by automobile; 20.5 percent walked or used roller skates; 13.9 percent commuted by bicycle. Buses in the Metropolis typically serve a secondary role, feeding passengers to and from train stations. Exceptions are long-distance bus services, buses in areas poorly served by rail (not many exist), and airport bus services for people with luggage. The [[Metropolitan Bureau of Transportation]] operates the [[ComMuni]] [[W:Light rail|light rail]] and bus service, mainly within the 12 boroughs, and is free, as is [[W:Free public transport|all public transit]]. All routes are required to feature non-step buses with a kneeling function to assist mobility-impaired users.

Bike lanes have been doubled across the city as {{W|street parking}} and {{W|parking minimum}}s have been eliminated and {{W|government incentives for plug-in electric vehicles}} are being repealed as part of the transition to substantially restrict private automobile usage. The Baliscan capital banned most polluting and {{W|private automobiles}} from key districts throughout the city progressively since the 1970s, and it has rapidly expanded the number of {{W|carfree zone}}s such as [[Mercadero]], [[Hachijoko]] and [[Takamaicán]].

Castejón is a major international air transport hub with the [[W:List of busiest city airport systems by passenger traffic|14th busiest airport system in the world]]. The city is served by two commercial international airports: [[Concordia International Airport|Castejón–Concordia Airport]] and [[Alunzaro International Airport|Jatiel–Alunzaro Airport]]. Together these airports recorded 85 million passengers in 2019. 
====Cycling====
[[File:Cas underground bike parking.png|left|thumb|Underground [[W:Bicycle parking station|bicycle parking station]] at [[Sol station]]]]
[[W:Cycling|Cycling]] in Castejón is the third-most popular mode of transport (after public transit and walking), and is widely considered a typical leisure activity within the capital city of Balisca. Following a national decline in the 1950s of levels of utility cycling, cycling as a mode of everyday transport within Balisca entered a slow regrowth in the 1960s before exploding in popularity following the [[October 76]] events and the [[Special Period]]. This growth has continued into the present - during the period from 1978 to 2022, the number of daily journeys made by bicycle in Castejón grew from 1.22 million to 8.16 million per day. The growth in cycling can partly be attributed to bicycle subsidies and the proliferation of {{W|bike sharing system}}s. 

The cycling boom was further buoyed by the 1997 launch by the Transport for Castejón (TfC) [[W:Bicycle sharing|bicycle sharing]] cooperative, ''[[Bicicomún]]'' ({{Literal translation|common bicycle}}). The system has 200,000 public bicycles distributed at 5,782 parking stations. By 2013, the cycle hire system had expanded rapidly, reaching underserved communities (such as [[Sástago]] and [[Arrabád]]) that had long complained of inaccessibility. Health impact analyses have shown that Castejón benefits significantly more from increased cycling and cycling infrastructure than other Abyalan cities.

Some 50 km (31 mi) of specially marked bus lanes are additionally free to be used by cyclists, with a protective barrier protecting against encroachments from vehicles. Cyclists have also been given the right to ride in both directions on certain one-way streets.
{{Clear}}
====Rapid transit and other rail====
{{Main|Castejón Metropolitan Rapid Transit|ComMuni}}

[[File:Llanito rail.png|left|thumb|The Castejón Metro is one of the most advanced and efficient transit systems in the world. Known for its extensive network, it is efficient and free, standing as a symbol of Castejón's rapid  growth post-WWII. Pictured: [[Castejón Metro Maraña Line|Maraña Line]]]]
[[File:ComMuni tram Ampurias.png|thumb|Public transit is the backbone of mobility in the Metropolis. Pictured is a [[W:Tram|tram]] of [[ComMuni]] in [[Ampurias]].]]
Castejón has a comprehensive urban railway network of 74 {{W|rapid transit}} ([[CMRT|Castejón Metro]]), {{W|light metro}} (ComMuni) and commuter lines that interconnects every barrio of the city and the surrounding areas of [[Yerba Buena]], [[Valparaíso]], [[León]], and northern [[Luzamontaña]]. It is one of the most extensive urban railway networks in the world. With almost 11 million passengers per weekday, the Castejón Metro is one of the busiest subway systems in the world and one of the largest in the world. In addition, in order to cope with the various modes of transport, Castejón's metropolitan government employs several mathematicians to coordinate the light and heavy rail, bus, and traffic schedules into one timetable. Rapid transit is essential in the Castejón Metropolis, with 65% of Castejón residents commuting to work in 2024 using rapid transit. This is similar to other major cities in Balisca, with less than 20% of commuters utilizing automobiles to get to their workplace.

Castejón's first train station, the predecessor to [[Mecerreyes Station|Mecerreyes]], appeared from 1854 as a home for the novelty Sol-Zacatín local line. Over the next ten years Balisca's developing rail network would give Castejón five (including the Solis station) national railway stations and two suburban lines, and from 1858 Castejón would become the designated centre of an ''estrela'' (star) spider-web of rail with reaches to (and through) all of Balisca's borders. 
[[File:Diretamente Kauã.png|thumb|An [[Alcántara]] ''Kauã'' EMU of [[Porciúncula–Jatiel high-speed rail line|Direto HSR]] transversing [[Tukupi]]]]
Additionally, Castejón is connected to every major city in [[Balisca]] by rail. For most large Baliscan cities, this is via the [[AVB|AVB high-speed network]], which has a normal operation speed of more than 300 km/h (186 mph) and is completely separated from the conventional rail network. Extensive [[W:Through service|through]] and [[W:Express rail|express services]] for seamless interconnections between certain lines are a major feature of the network; the ''Cisalpujarra'' service run integrates track of 6 separate and independent operators. [[W:Suburban rail|Suburban rail]] operations and subway lines are also typically integrated. Frequent and high-capacity suburban trains from the suburbs commonly continue directly into the subway network to serve central Castejón, often emerging on the other side of the city to serve another commune's surface suburban lines, behaving similar to an [[W:S-bahn|S-bahn]] network. [[Coruxón station]] is the busiest train station in the Algarves by passenger throughput, with 2.8 million people using the station per day in 2024.

Trains had historically been extremely crowded at peak travel times, with people being pushed into trains by so-called ''[[W:Oshiya|empujadores]]'' ("pushers"), which was common in the boom eras of the 1950s-1970s and again in the 1980s-2000s. Upgrades on the Metropolis' railways are chiefly focused on improving services and [[W:Grade-separation|grade-separating]] lines.

==== Water transport ====
[[File:555 Almendros.png|left|thumb|329x329px|[[W:Water taxi|Water taxis]] on the [[Tistaina Canal]] between [[Ampurias]] and [[Expansión]]]]
Although much diminished from its past prominence, water-based transport still plays an important role in Castejón and the immediate upstream and downstream communes. Several [[W:Water bus|water buses]] serve commuters daily. Passenger ferries and water taxis operate extensively across Salto Bay and along the Alagón River, serving as an integral part of Castejón's [[Transport in Concordia|free public transit]] system, operating within the [[ComMuni]] transit federation. These services connect various boroughs and communal districts within the metropolis, easing congestion on land-based routes and offering scenic commuting options. Many of the [[Salto Islands]] are accessible only by water routes and helicopters, as they are too small or undulating for a landing ground, making these ships the primary means of inter-island transport.
[[File:Metropolitan Ferry with skyline.png|thumb|A [[ComMuni|Metropolitan Ferry]] with the background of the [[Mercadero]] district of the [[Ampurias]] [[Boroughs of Castejón|borough]]]]
Castejón, prior to the 1990s, operated extensive port facilities on Salto Bay, particularly in [[Sástago]] and [[Llanito]]. From the beginning of the 1990s, these operations were gradually shifted to the [[Jatiel|Port of Jatiel]], and today its cargo and industrial sections handle significant volumes of goods, crucial for Balisca's position as a major [[Internet in Balisca|IT exporter]] and a hub for complex global supply chains. This relocation built upon the precedent set by the [[1992 Salto Bay referendum|1992 referendum]] that banned commercial fishing in Salto Bay, further signaling a prioritization of ecological health and public space. The removal of industrial shipping from the bay significantly reduced water, air, and noise pollution within the metropolitan core. This has led to a marked improvement in the bay's water quality, a revitalization of its marine ecosystem, and the expansion of pristine public waterfronts.

===Technology===
{{Main|Science and technology in Balisca}}
{{See also|LinkCastejón|LinkBalisca}}
Castejón has been described as one of the world's most wired cities, and was ranked first in the [[Western Hemisphere]] in technology readiness by PwC's Cities of Opportunity report. In the 21st century, Castejón has rapidly developed a very technologically advanced infrastructure system.

Castejón is among the world leaders in [[W:Internet access|internet connectivity]], being a major node of international networking, which has the the Algarves' highest fibre-optic broadband penetration and highest global average internet speeds of 26.1 Mbit/s. Beginning in 2022, Castejón has provided free Wi-Fi access in outdoor spaces through a S/490 million (ε367 million) project with internet access at 10,430 parks, streets and other public places. Additionally, in partnership with communal [[W:Waste management|waste management]] services, the city has installed sidewalk trash bins equipped with [[W:Wifi hotspot|wifi hotspots]].

Internet speeds in some ''multos altos'' complexes reach up to 52.5Gbit/s through the nationwide [[Cielo Íntegro]] ("complete sky"), a [[W:Satellite internet constellation|satellite internet constellation]] developed [[W:Utility cooperative|cooperatively]] and launched in 2023. Though the average standard consists of 100 Mbit/s services, providers nationwide are rapidly rolling out 1Gbit/s connections at the equivalent of US$25 per month. In addition, the city is served by the [[AVB|AVB high-speed rail]] and the Castejón Metro, which provides [[W:4G LTE|4G LTE]], WiFi and [[W:Digital multimedia broadcasting|DMB]] inside metro cars. The city has been gradually rolling out [[W:5G|5G]] connectivity via cooperation with communal entities, beginning in June 2018.

