Excluding the outlying surface of [[Salto Bay]], Castejón roughly covers a square measuring about {{Convert|6,038.4|km2|sqmi|abbr=on}} in area. The metropolis' last major annexation of outlying territories in 1975 resulted it present-day square borders. The metropolis contains 523 municipal parks, 86 of which are historic, 45 of which are thematic ([[W:Botanical garden|botanical]]), 225 of which are [[W:Urban park|urban]], and six of which are forest. They range from [[W:linear park|linear]] and [[W:Pocket park|vest-pocket parks]] to large recreation areas. The urban parks alone cover 14% of the city (549.7 ha or 1,358.3 acres). The total park surface grows about 10 ha (25 acres) per year, with a proportion of 18.1 m<sup>2</sup> (195 sq ft) of park area per inhabitant.

Of Castejón's parks, the [[Jacarandá Forest|Bosque de Jacarandá]] is the largest, with ? ha located on the mountain of the same name. It is followed by [[Parc de la Confederación]] (which occupies the site of the old military citadel and which houses the [[Baliscan Cabildo|Congress building]], the [[Castejón Conservatory]], and several museums); 31 ha or 76.6 acres including the zoo), the Guinardó Park (19 ha or 47.0 acres), Parc Güell (designed by Antoni Gaudí; 17.2 ha or 42.5 acres), Oreneta Castle Park (also 17.2 ha or 42.5 acres), Diagonal Mar Park (13.3 ha or 32.9 acres, inaugurated in 2002), [[Nou Barris Central Park]] (13.2 ha or 32.6 acres), [[Olympic Park (Castejón)|Olympic Park]] and [[Gistaín Park]] (both 11.9 ha or 29.4 acres), the Labyrinth Park (9.10 ha or 22.5 acres), named after the garden maze it contains. There are also several smaller parks, for example, [[Quinta de Viñas]], the [[Parc de La Aigües]] (which is enclosed by [[Expansión]]; (2 ha or 4.9 acres).

===Sanitation and water management===
{{See also|Baliscan water wars}}{{multiple image
| direction         = vertical
| align             = left
| width1            = 265
| width2            = 265
| footer            = 
| image1            = Aipi reservoir.png
| caption1          = [[Aipi]], a {{W|graben}} and artificial reservoir/water system in Bayara that has provided the majority of the Metropolis' water demand since 1968
| image2            = Castejon floodgates.png
| caption2          = A floodgate in [[Hisbanya]], connected to the [[Metropolitan Underground Discharge System]]
}}

The [[Metropolitan Council of Castejón|administration of the Castejón Metropolis]] has long recognized access to [[W:Freshwater|freshwater]] as an increasingly pressing issue and has sought to emphasize [[W:Water conservation|conservation]]. Water access is universal and of high quality, though the region is projected to face significant water-stress by 2040. To circumvent this, the Metropolitan Utilities Board has implemented the "three metropolitan taps" strategy – water imported from the surrounding Araucarian regions and Bayara, [[W:Rainwater harvesting|urban rainwater catchments]] and [[W:Reclaimed water|reclaimed water]] (NEWater). The Metropolis' approach does not rely only on physical infrastructure, but it also emphasizes proper legislation and enforcement, water pricing, public education as well as research and development. 
[[File:Kasukabe2006 06 07.JPG|thumb|A concrete containment silo of the [[Metropolitan Underground Discharge System]] (MUDS)]]
The Metropolitan Council has declared that it will be water self-sufficient by the time its 1950 long-term water supply agreement with Araucaria expires in 2024. Currently, the Castejón Metropolis exercises legal jurisdiction over four {{W|reservoir}} {{w|exclaves}}, completely surrounded by [[Araucaria]] and [[Bayara]], which collectively cover about 60% of the city's water needs. According to official forecasts, water demand in Castejón is expected to double from 7.2 to 14.4 billion litres; 7.2 to 14.4 million cubic meters per day between 2020 and 2060. The increase is expected to come primarily from non-domestic water use, which accounted for 55% of water demand in 2010 and is expected to account for 70% of demand in 2060. By that time, water demand is expected to be met by reclaimed water at the tune of 50% and by desalination accounting for 30%, compared to only 20% supplied by internal catchments. 

A huge system of [[W:Floodgate|floodgates]], [[W:Levee|dikes]] and tunnels was constructed following the devastating flooding triggered by the arrival of [[sulcarano Amara]] in the summer of 1983. The purpose is to manage heavy rain, typhonic rain, and river floods. The system of Castejón has an underground floodwater diversion facility known as the [[Metropolitan Underground Discharge System]] (MUDS). It took 15 years to build and was completed in 2000. MUDS is a 9.4 km long system of tunnels, 22 meters underground, with 70 meter tall cylindrical tanks. During floods, excess water is collected from rivers and drained into Salto Bay.

==Culture and society==
{{Main|Culture of Balisca}}

{{See also|Baliscan values}}
[[File:Capitoline Hill streetart.jpg|thumb|Street Art and Graffiti in [[Capitoline Hill]], during the [[2021 Baliscan civil unrest|2021 social revolution]].]]
Castejón's culture stems from the area's 700 years of permanent settlement, particularly it's role as Balisca's [[W:Primate city|primate city]] since the 19th century. Castejón has historically been a cultural center of reference in the world. To a greater extent than the rest of Balisca, where their respective native languages is more dominant, Castejón is a multilingual city, although [[Baliscano language|Baliscano]] is the [[W:Working language|working language]] of the metropolitan government. Since the [[Baliscan Revolution]], the city's long history of [[W:Permissive culture|permissive culture]] has influenced the rest of Balisca, both by recovering works from the past and by stimulating the creation of new works. Castejón today is considered a prominent international hub of highly active and diverse cultural life with {{W|street art}}, concerts, festivals, {{W|fashion week}}, [[w:List of concert halls|concert halls]], {{W|drug tourism|drug}} and {{W|sex tourism}}, [[List of museums in Castejón|museums]], and high-value architectural heritage. 

Castejón has long been a subject of interest dating back to [[Colonial Balisca|colonial times]] and has maintained a reputation as "the city of no gods", and a place of opportunity for "a fresh start" for migrants of all backgrounds. Despite the confederation-wide [[Special Period|economic stagnation of the 1970s]], the metropolis remained the top destination for both domestic and international migration. An atmosphere of hopefulness and determination emerged in the early 1980s following the annexation of [[Trinacria]], resulting in an influx of new popular music and [[W:Counterculture|countercultural thought]]. Trinacria in particular has since gained a reputation as a haven for counterculture.

Millions of diverse migrants have come to call the city home; including thousands of influential artists, designers, developers and political activists flocked to the city, bringing with them various counter and sub-cultures and diverse political views. Stemming from these initial population movements into the city and across the remainder of Balisca, it became a hotspot of the ''[[Desconexión]]'' ("unplug") subculture. As such, Castejón has developed a very community-oriented culture, with {{W|solidarity}} and [[W:Communitarianism|communitarianism]] held to a very high degree.  

Castejón's success is still seen as a symbol of Balisca’s unique economic style, which tends to generate idiosyncratic technology, social, entertainment, and economic fads and booms and related busts. The city competes with [[Concepción]], [[Yerba Buena]] and [[Axarquía]] as the predominant centers of popular Baliscan art, music, and fashion. Additionally, Castejón was the birthplace of [[Sujo|''sujo'']] ({{Literal translation|dirt|obscene}}), a form of dance music, characterized by a fusion of Latin rhythms, dancehall, and hip-hop or rap. 

=== Social spaces, nightclubs and festivals ===
[[File:Castejon cannabis cafe.png|left|thumb|A [[W:Coffeeshop (Netherlands)|cannabis cafe]] in Llanito; Castejón has the [[Cannabis in Balisca|highest number of such cafes]] per capita in Balisca.]]
Castejón's social life has been celebrated as one of the most diverse, lively, and vibrant of its kind. Pariticiarly during the 1970s and 80s, the city became a major center of [[Drug policy of Balisca|Baliscan drug culture]] and [[W:Sex tourism|sex tourism]], alongside being a center for punk music and culture. The barrios [[Navajas]] and [[Arrabád]] gained notoriety during this period. Throughout the 1980s, people in their 20s from all over the world, particularly those in [[Abyala]], made Castejón's club scene a premier [[W:nightlife|nightlife destination]]. During the [[Special Period]] many historic buildings were [[W:Self-managed social center|occupied and re-built by young squatters and anarchists]] and became a fertile ground for underground and [[W:Counterculture|counterculture]] gatherings. The outer boroughs are home to many nightclubs, including the [[El Arcoíris]], [[El Salón Dorado]] and [[La Esquina Mágica]]. [[Chicoño]] and other locations are known for their [[W:Group sex|sexually uninhibited parties]].

Clubs typically don't close at fixed time during the weekends, and most parties last well into the morning or even all weekend. Several venues have become a popular stage for the [[W:Neo-Burlesque|Neo-Burlesque]] scene. 

Castejón has a long and vibrant history regarding [[W:Queer culture|queer culture]], and is an important center for the modern [[LGBTQ rights in Balisca|LGBTQ rights movement]] and [[W:LGBTQ culture|LGBTQ culture]]. [[W:Gay bar|Queer bars]], [[W:gay bathhouse|gay bathhouses]], [[W:Drag shows|drag shows]] and [[W:Dance hall|dance halls]] have operated freely as early as the 1880s, and the [[W:List of LGBTQ periodicals|first gay magazine]], ''Lavanda'', entered circulation in 1896. By the 1920s, gays, lesbians and "[[W:Travesti (gender identity)|travesti]]" had an unprecedented visibility. Today, in addition to a near universally accepting atmosphere in the wider club scene, the city again has a huge number of queer clubs and festivals. The most famous and largest festivals are Castejón Pride, the [[W:Folsom Street Fair|Folsom Street Fair]], [[Festival de la Flor y el Amor]], [[Festival de la Humanitá]] and [[W:Afropunk|Afropunk]].

=== Community cats ===
[[File:Balisca flood of 1803 painting.png|thumb|275x275px|''El guardián de los gatos'' ([[Xabier Sánchez]]), depicting residents of [[Llanito]] attempting to save feral street cats from rising floodwaters as they seek shelter during the [[Flood of 1803]].]]
The city hosts a sizable [[W:Community cat|community cat]] population, with estimates ranging from several hundred thousand to over two million community cats, including [[W:Working cat|working cats]] (i.e. [[W:Library cat|library cats]], [[W:Bodega cat|shop cats]]). Residents almost universally view street animals as communally-owned pets, rather than traditional strays, and the country as a whole has a blanket no-kill, no capture policy.
[[File:Statue of Ónix.jpg|left|thumb|Ónix ("[[W:Onyx|Onyx]]") was a popular [[W:Tuxedo cat|tuxedo cat]] with residents of the [[Sol|Sol neighborhood]], known for his friendliness and his white paws.]]
The wide prevalence of cats in the city can be connected back to colonial times. The vast majority of Castejón's buildings at the time were made of wood, which gave shelter and enabled the proliferation of the mouse and rat populations. These problems were exacerbated by unsanitary conditions tied to population influxes and overcrowding following the [[Baliscan Civil War]]. This made cats' presence a necessity in the city, and their population swelled throughout the 17th and 18th centuries. The widely positive attitude towards cats is shared across Balisca, and is attributed to [[Moro people|Moro]]-[[Islam in Balisca|Muslim influence]] from [[Andalus]] and the enslaved [[Fezzan Baliscans|Fezzans]] (see {{W|Islam and cats}}), the first of whom arrived in the 16th century.

===Cuisine===
{{See also|Baliscan cuisine}}
Today, due to Castejón's cosmopolitan population, every Baliscan [[W:Regional cuisine|regional cuisine]] and almost every type of [[W:National dish|national cuisine]] in the world along with a diverse array of [[W:Fusion cuisine|fusion]] can be found in Castejón; in 2024, the city had more than 30,000 restaurants.

Castejón's culinary reputation has its basis in the diverse ethnic origins of its inhabitants. Balisca's ethno-cultural diversities have produced distinctive cuisines, much like regional varieties of wine. These mingled with Castejón's own regional traditions. In its beginnings, Castejón's culinary development owed much to the 19th-century organization of a railway system that had the city as a center, making the capital a focal point for migration from Balisca's many different regions and gastronomical cultures. This reputation continues through today in a cultural diversity that has since spread to a worldwide level thanks to Castejón's continued reputation for culinary finesse and further immigration from increasingly distant climes. Immigrants from former Volisanian colonies have also infused Baliscan cuisine with their own traditions, originating in [[Volistraya]] and East Fosia, [[North Kaftia|North]], [[Central Kaftia|Central]] and [[West Kaftia]], alongside other countries in [[Abyala]].

Castejón's restaurants reflect this diversity, with menus carrying traditional regional cuisine, fusions of various culinary influences, or innovating in the leading edge of new techniques, such as {{W|molecular gastronomy}}. Castejón's food shops also have a solid reputation for supplying quality specialized culinary products and supplies, reputations that are often built up over generations.

===Fashion===
===Popular culture===
[[File:Navajas night.png|left|thumb|[[Navajas]], in the [[Limón]] borough, is the most popular area for local and international fans of Baliscan anime, manga, and games.|335x335px]]
[[File:Manchitas.png|thumb|295x295px|[[Manchitas]], the [[Mascots of Baliscan regions|regional mascot]] of the Castejón Metropolis ([[W:Magpie tanager|Magpie tanager]])]]
As the largest population center in Balisca and the site of the country's largest broadcasters and studios, Castejón is frequently the setting for many [[Cinema of Balisca|Baliscan movies]], television shows, animated series ([[W:Anime|anime]]), [[W:Web comics|web comics]], [[W:Light novels|light novels]], [[W:Video games|video games]], and comic books ([[W:Manga|manga]]). 

In the late 1980s, the individual was the focus of leisure activities, such as excursions to parks or shopping districts. The Metropolis and Balisca as a whole are known to value communal interaction and prioritizes mental well-being. Described as having a "{{W|All work and no play makes Jack a dull boy|all play and no work}}" mentality, the Metropolites has been known to seek entertainment whenever they can and maximize {{W|leisure}}. It is common to see Baliscan commuters riding the train to work, enjoying their favorite books or shows, or listening through earphones to the latest in {{W|popular music}}. A wide variety of types of popular entertainment are available.  

There is a large selection of music, films, and the products of a huge animation industry, among other forms of entertainment, from which to choose. Game centers, bowling alleys, and karaoke are popular hangout places for teens while older people may go to bars, clubs, {{W|love hotel}}s or street events which are increasingly common. Popular cinema directors have often turned to Castejón and the wider [[Alpujarra]] as a backdrop for movies set in Balisca. Post-revolution examples include ''[[tba|A Baliscan Roadtrip]]'', [[Canto a la Libertad|''Canto a la Libertad'']], ''[[Sunrise in Castejón]]'' and the [[La Última Llama]] (LUL) film [[Ciudad Pecado|''Ciudad Pecado'']]; more recent examples include ''[[Castejónes]]'', ''[[City of No Gods]]'', [[El Mar Sin Orilla|''El Mar Sin Orilla'']], ''[[Babel (film)|Babel]]'', and ''[[The New Babylon]]''. 

Baliscan author [[Ximena Pineda]] has based some of her alternative history novels in Castejón (including ''[[The Palace of Spice (novel)|The Palace of Spice]]''), and [[Elías Benítez (author)|Elías Benítez]]'s first two novels ([[El Fantasma del Abrazo|''El Fantasma del Abrazo'']] and [[El Jardín de las Cenizas|''El Jardín de las Cenizas'']]) featured the city. Contemporary [[W:Urban realism|urban realist]] painter [[Ricardo Almazán]] spent 10 years living in Castejón as an artist, exploring it's {{W|unitary urbanism}} to create a body of work depicting the city's crowded streets and public spaces.

