[[File:Yoruba figurines at Sanyatunji Park.png|left|thumb|[[W:Yoruba art|Òrìṣà-Ifá figurines]] at [[Sanyatunji Park]], representing the twin [[W:Ibeji|Jimaguas]] [[W:Orisha|orisha]]]]
Baliscan law bans the establishment of an {{W|state religion|official religion}} at any administrative level, and prevents the government from involving itself in the private religious affairs of Baliscan citizens. Additionally, It allows the right to believe what a person, group, or {{W|religion}} wishes, but it does not allow the right to practice the religion or belief openly and outwardly in a public manner, a central facet of {{W|religious freedom}}. Since the implementation of {{W|secularism}}, the Baliscan government has co-opted the Hersatian principle of {{W|laïcité}}, in which the State does not recognize any official ''religion''. Instead, it merely recognizes certain religious organizations, according to formal legal criteria that do not address religious doctrine. In return, religious organizations are to refrain from involvement in the State's policy-making or any activity that could be interpreted as political in a court of law.

In Balisca, "freedom of religious belief" is used as a more broad replacement for both "freedom of religion" and "freedom from religion". The term "belief" is considered inclusive of all forms of {{W|irreligion}}, including {{W|atheism}}, {{W|antitheism}} and {{W|humanism}}. The practice of religion is now legally considered a private matter throughout society and the state.

Islam was introduced in the Baliscan territory following the arrival of explorers and colonists on behalf of the [[Crown of Volisania]] the late 15th to early 16th century, with Muslim merchants establishing some of the first [[W:Factory (trading post)|factories]] in the New World. Renewed religious conflict in Illypnia and the Mesogean basin resulted in many of these Muslim merchant communities being forced to choose [[Expulsion of the Moros|between conversion or expulsion]] by their host countries, with many departing for Abyala. Significantly larger numbers of enslaved Kaftian Muslims were brought to the Baliscan territory following the beginning of the [[Kaftian slave trade]]. Historically, an estimated 60% of slaves brought to Balisca from [[West Kaftia|West]]/[[Central Kaftia]] were Muslims. They were overwhelmingly literate in contrast to many of the slave owners.

Following the outbreak of the [[Maroon Wars]], significant number of [[Slavery in Balisca|enslaved people]] rebelled across the [[Marañón basin]], leading to a violent campaign of self-liberation across the agricultural heartland. Thousands escaped, some with the help of the [[Catharism|Catharist]] community which was vehemently opposed to slavery. This resistance eventually coalesced into the [[Araucarian Passage]]. Following the [[Baliscan Civil War]] and the relocation of the capital to Castejón, hundreds of thousands of people displaced by the fighting settled in the [[Alpujarra]].

==International relations==
Castejón is classified as an [[w:Global city|Alpha+ World City]], according to the [[W:Loughborough University|Loughborough University]] group's (GaWC) 2018 inventory. It has consistently ranked as one of the world's most livable cities.
